To address these challenges, model compression techniques like quantization \cite{zafrir2019q8bert} and pruning \cite{kwon2022fast} have been proposed. Quantization reduces computational requirements by lowering numerical bitwidths, though it can lead to performance degradation. Despite these advances, quantized models often remain inefficient for edge devices and lack effective acceleration on existing hardware, necessitating specialized designs.

Binary transformers, an extreme form of quantization, represent all weights and activations using values like \((-1, 1)\) or \((0, 1)\). These models significantly reduce computational complexity, model size, and communication bandwidth while replacing floating-point operations with efficient bitwise calculations\cite{zhirubnn, fracbnn, qiao2022two, 10025006}. This approach enables faster execution and lower power consumption, making binary transformers a promising solution for edge deployment.

