\subsubsection{Real Binary Attention}
BiBERT \cite{qin2022bibert} and BiT \cite{liu2022bit} followed Eq. (\ref{alg:bsoftmax}) to implement softmax in their binary attentions. This approach overlooks hardware-level overhead of softmax, leading to suboptimal computation speed, hardware costs, and power efficiency, making it impractical for edge deployment. Furthermore, the use of softmax requires exponential computation and produces non-binary values in the attention mechanism, preventing BiT from achieving a fully binarized transformer model and complicating efficient hardware acceleration. In detail, BiT calculates a vanilla attention score (including softmax operation) and applies elastic binarization as Eq. (\ref{alg:bit}), effectively redistributing the attention score to binary domain. 
\begin{equation} \label{alg:bsoftmax}
   \text{Softmax}(z_i) = \frac{\exp(z_i)}{\sum_{j=1}^{n} \exp(z_j)}
\end{equation}
% \begin{equation} \label{alg:battention}
%     Attention(Q_b, K_b, V_b) = Softmax \left( \frac{Q_b K_b^\top}{\sqrt{d_k}} \right) V_b
% \end{equation}  

\begin{equation} \label{alg:bit}
    Att\_prob_{BiT} = \text{clip}\left(\text{round}\left(\frac{\text{Softmax}\left(\frac{Q_b K_b^\top}{\sqrt{d_k}}\right)}{\alpha}\right), 0, 1\right)
\end{equation}
where $\text{clip}(\cdot, 0, 1)$ clamps input elements into  $[0,1]$, and $\text{round}(\cdot)$ rounds input to the nearest integer.
However, both the softmax operation and the elastic binarization function rely on many floating-point operations, which dominate the computational complexity of binary models and significantly compromise the hardware acceleration.

To overcome these limitations, we propose Shifted Polarized Softmax (SPS) as a direct replacement for softmax and binarization in attention computation. SPS generates binary-state outputs directly, without relying on any floating-point arithmetic, thereby eliminating the need for a separate binarization step and further improving hardware efficiency.
% Its reliance on floating-point operations further undermines the potential speedup offered by binarization, adding latency and inefficiency. Moreover, floating-point operations place additional demands on edge devices, which are often resource-constrained. Consequently, these models fail to fully utilize the benefits of binarization, achieving only marginal hardware performance improvements despite reductions in model size and complexity.


% \begin{equation} \label{alg:bit}
%     Att\_prob_b = \left\lfloor\text{clip}\left(\frac{\text{Softmax}\left(\frac{Q_b K_b^\top}{\sqrt{d_k}}\right)}{\alpha}, 0, 1\right)\right\rceil
% \end{equation}


\subsubsection{Shifted Polarized Softmax (SPS)} \label{sec:sps}

%% We observe that BiT \cite{liu2022bit} achieves binarization by redistributing attention outputs to the (0,1) space as shown in Eq. (\ref{alg:bit}). This inspires us to design a hardware-friendly approximation to softmax, named Shifted Polarized Softmax (SPS). 

% To overcome the inefficiency of softmax, we propose a hardware-friendly approximation of softmax, named Shifted Polarized Softmax (SPS). 
% Conventional softmax scales inputs into a continuous probability distribution, while elastic binarization maps it back to binary as shown in Eq. (\ref{alg:bit}). In contrast, the SPS function directly polarizes values into binary states using a learnable threshold, capturing softmax’s purpose—emphasizing stronger signals—without continuous scaling, as shown in Eq. (\ref{alg:sps}). As noted in \cite{qin2022bibert}, maximizing the entropy of binarized attention scores is crucial for effective binary representation. To achieve this, SPS replaces Eq. (\ref{alg:bit}) by training a dynamic, head-wise threshold \( T \) to mitigate the loss of softmax normalization. SPS approximates softmax while reducing computational overhead, making it ideal for hardware acceleration. 
Conventional softmax scales input values into a continuous probability distribution over the range (0,1) and the quantization function rescale the distribution back to binary form, the SPS function instead polarizes values directly into binary states based on a learnable threshold, capturing the core purpose of softmax—emphasizing stronger signals—without the need for continuous probabilistic scaling, as shown in Eq. (\ref{alg:sps}). 
\begin{equation} \label{alg:sps}
   % Att\_prob_b = \text{SPS}\left(\frac{Q_b K_b^\top}{\sqrt{d_k}}\right), \ 
   \text{SPS}(z) = 
   \begin{cases}
       1, & \text{if } z \geq \lambda_{i,k} \\
       0, & \text{otherwise}
   \end{cases} 
\end{equation}
where $i$ indicate $i^{th}$ attention block and $k$ represent $K^{th}$ attention head in the attention block.
\begin{equation} \label{alg:cod-bt}
    Att\_prob_{SPS} = \text{SPS}\left(\frac{Q_b K_b^\top}{\sqrt{d_k}}\right)
\end{equation}
As discussed in \cite{qin2022bibert}, for the attention mechanism to capture crucial elements in the binary representation, maximizing the information entropy of binarized attention score is a must and a binarized attention score matrix can serve the purpose. Hence, we propose SPS to replace Eq. (\ref{alg:bit}) by search for a series of head-wise thresholds $\lambda \in h\times n$ that further compensate the information loss for not having softmax normalization for attention score. SPS approximates the effects of softmax plus elastic binarization function while significantly reducing computational overhead, making it more compatible with hardware constraints.
% A comparison of the output distributions between softmax and SPS is illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:SPS}.
Our Final attention probability can be found as Eq. (\ref{alg:cod-bt}). 

