\section{Fast and oblivious Runge--Kutta gCQ}\label{sec:algfi}
\subsection{Algorithm for the convolution}\label{sec:algconv}
For efficient implementation, we extend the algorithm proposed in \cite{BanLo19} to the general mesh \eqref{mesh}. Denoting
\[\fv_n=\left(f(t_{n-1}+c_i\tau_n)\right)_{i=1}^s,\quad 1\le n\le N.\]
Following  \cite{GuoLo} and reacalling the notation in \eqref{Un_stage}, we split the gCQ  approximation into two parts:
\begin{equation}\label{gCQshort_split}
	\Uv_n = \left[K\left( \partial^{\Delta}_t \right)\fv \right]_n  =\sum\limits_{j=\max\left(1, n-n_0+1\right)}^{n}\Wv_{n,j} \fv_j+ \sum\limits_{j=1}^{n-n_0}\Wv_{n,j} \fv_j = \Sv_n^{loc}+\Sv_n^{his},
\end{equation}
with $n_0$ a fixed moderate constant, say $n_0=5$ and which can also be equal to 1. Using the definition of $\Wv_{n,j}$ in \eqref{gCQ_w}, we have
\begin{align}
	\nonumber
	\Sv_n^{loc}&=\frac{1}{2\pi i}\int_{\mathcal{C}}  K(z) \sum\limits_{j=\max\left(1, n-n_0+1\right)}^{n}\left(\prod_{l=j+1}^{n} \left(\Rv(\tau_lz)\ev_s^\top\right)\right) \left  (\tau_j\Av(\Iv-\tau_jz\Av)^{-1}\fv_j \right) \,dz\\
	\label{Snloc}
	&:=\frac{1}{2\pi i}\int_{\mathcal{C}} K(z)\Qv_n^{loc}(z) \,dz,
\end{align}
where
\begin{equation}\label{recursion_loc}
	\Qv^{loc}_{n}(z)=\Rv(\tau_nz) \ev_s^\top\Qv^{loc}_{n-1}(z)+\tau_n\Av(\Iv-\tau_n\Av z)^{-1}\fv_n,\qquad \Qv^{loc}_{(n-n0)_+}(z)=\mathbf{0}.
\end{equation}
To approximate the local term, we select $\mathcal{C}$ as a circle in the right half of the complex plane, enclosing the poles of the integrand, and parameterized by using elliptic functions. The same idea appears in \cite{GuoLo} for Euler based gCQ, but we introduce the adjustments in $\mathcal{C}$ indicated for Runge--Kutta methods in \cite{LoSau16}. The radius of $\mathcal{C}$ is then given by
\[M^{loc}=5\max\left\{\left\Vert  \left({\rm{spec}}\Av^{-1}\right )\right\Vert\right\}/\min\limits_{\max\left(1, n-n_0+1\right)\le j\le \max(n,n_0)}\{\tau_j\},\]
and centered at $M+m/10$, where
\[m^{loc}=\min\left\{\Re\left  ({\rm{spec}}\left (\Av^{-1}\right)\right )\right\}/\max\limits_{\max\left(1, n-n_0+1\right)\le j\le \max(n,n_0)}\{\tau_j\}.\]
Subsequently, the quadrature rule for the local term can be formulated as follows
\begin{equation}\label{Sloc_quad}
	\Sv^{loc}_n \approx \sum_{l=1}^{N_{Q}^{loc}} w_l z_l^{-\alpha} \Qv_n^{loc}(z_l).
\end{equation}
We notice that the circle $\mathcal{C}$ is fixed for the first $n_0$ steps, until we compute $\Uv_{n_0}$, and then it changes at every time step $n>n_0$.

For the history part in \eqref{gCQshort_split} we notice that
\begin{align}
	\nonumber
	\Sv_n^{his}&=\int_{0}^\infty G(x) \sum\limits_{j=1}^{n-n_0}\left(\prod_{l=j+1}^{n} \left(\Rv(-\tau_lx)\ev_s^\top\right)\right) \left  (\tau_j\Av(\Iv+\tau_j x\Av)^{-1} \fv_j\right) \,dx\\
	\label{S_hisint}
	&:= \int_{0}^\infty G(x)\left(\prod_{l=n-n_0+1}^{n} \left(\Rv(-\tau_lx)\ev_s^\top\right)\right)\Qv^{his}_{n-n_0}(x) \,dx,
\end{align}
where
\[\Qv^{his}_{n-n_0}(x)= \sum\limits_{j=1}^{n-n_0}\left(\prod_{l=j+1}^{n-n_0} \left(\Rv(-\tau_lx)\ev_s^\top\right)\right) \left  (\tau_j\Av(\Iv+\tau_j x\Av)^{-1}\fv_j \right),\]
satisfying
\begin{equation}\label{recursion_his}
	\Qv^{his}_{n-n_0}(x)=\Rv(-\tau_{n-n_0}x)\ev_s^\top\Qv^{his}_{n-n_0-1}(x)+\tau_{n-n_0}\Av (\Iv+\tau_{n-n_0}\Av x)^{-1}\fv_{n-n_0},\quad \Qv^{his}_{0}(x)=\mathbf{0}.
\end{equation}
In Section~\ref{sec:num_test}, different quadrature rules are employed depending on the form of \( G(x) \). When \( G(x)  \) takes the form  \eqref{Gx_fracint}, the quadrature nodes \( x_l \) and weights \( \varpi_l \) are computed by using the oblivious Gauss quadrature method proposed in \cite{BanLo19,GuoLo}. For other types of kernels, the trapezoidal rule is used instead, see Example~\ref{ex_genker}. A more optimized quadrature for each specific kernel might be arranged, but we do not pursue this issue in the present paper. In any case, this yields an approximation of the form
\begin{align}
	\label{Shis_quad}
	\Sv^{his}_n \approx \sum\limits_{l=1}^{N^{his}_Q} \varpi_l G(x_l)\left(\prod_{j=n-n_0+1}^{n} \left(\Rv(-\tau_jx_l)\ev_s^\top\right)\right) \Qv_{n-n_0}^{his}(x_l).
\end{align}
Thus, we obtain the approximation
\begin{equation}\label{gCQ_appInt}
	\left[K\left( \partial^{\Delta}_t\right)\fv\right]_n  \approx \sum_{l=1}^{N_{Q}^{loc}} w_l K(z_l) \Qv_n^{loc}(z_l)+\sum\limits_{l=1}^{N^{his}_Q} \varpi_lG(x_l)\left(\prod\limits_{j=n-n_0+1}^{n}\left(\Rv(-\tau_jx_l)\ev_s^\top\right)\right)\Qv_{n-n_0}(x_l).
\end{equation}
In our numerical experiments, we set
\begin{equation*}
	n_0=\min\left(5,N\right).
\end{equation*}
Applying the formula from \cite[Corollary 16]{LoSau15apnum}, we choose
\begin{equation}\label{NQloc}
	N^{\mathrm{loc}}_Q = \left\lceil n_0^{\max\left\{1,\, \frac{1}{2} \log(M_{\mathrm{loc}})/\log(m_{\mathrm{loc}}) \right\}} \log(n_0)\left( \log(n_0) + \log(1/\mathrm{tol}) \right) \right\rceil
\end{equation}
in the quadrature rule \eqref{Sloc_quad}, where \(\mathrm{tol}\) denotes the prescribed tolerance for the quadrature used in both \eqref{Sloc_quad} and \eqref{Shis_quad}. The total computational cost of the algorithm is \(O(n_0 N_Q^{\mathrm{loc}} + (N - n_0) N_Q^{\mathrm{his}})\), while the memory requirement is \(O(N_Q^{\mathrm{loc}} + N_Q^{\mathrm{his}})\). In Figure~\ref{fig:num_quadcomp} we compare the total number of quadrature nodes required to approximate the fractional integral in Example~\ref{ex_fracint} on graded meshes, by using the algorithm in \cite{LoSau16} and by using the fast and oblivious algorithm in this Section. As shown, the algorithm in this Section significantly improves both efficiency and memory usage compared to the original gCQ  implementation in \cite{LoSau15apnum}. Specifically, the total number of quadrature nodes, this is $N_Q^{loc}+N_Q^{his}$, required by our fast algorithm grows like \(O(\log N)\) on the graded mesh \eqref{gmesh}, implying an overall computational complexity of \(O(N \log N)\) and a memory usage of \(O(\log N)\). This growth turns out to be due only to the evaluation of the history term $\Sv^{his}_n$, since in all the cases depicted in Figure~\ref{fig:num_quadcomp} we keep fixed $N_Q^{loc}=273$, which guarantees the high tolerance requirement $tol=10^{-14}$ in \eqref{NQloc}. 

We emphasize that thanks to the improvements in this Section we are able to choose very strongly graded meshes, with grading exponent $\gamma$ up to $20$. For the original implementation of the gCQ method in \cite{LoSau15apnum,LoSau16}, the complexity starts to become unaffordable in practice for $\gamma>2$.

