\begin{example}\label{ex_genker}
Consider the Runge--Kutta based gCQ  approximation \eqref{gCQ_appInt} for convolutions \eqref{conv} with the following two kernels:
\begin{equation}\label{k_genker}
	k_a(t) = -\frac{d}{dt} E_{\alpha,1}(-t^\alpha), \quad k_b(t) = \frac{t^{\alpha-1}}{\Gamma(\alpha)} e^{-t},
\end{equation}
acting on the function
\begin{equation*}
	f(t) = t^\beta.
\end{equation*}
\end{example}
The Laplace transforms of the kernels in \eqref{k_genker} are given by
\[
K_a(z) = \frac{1}{z^\alpha + 1}, \quad K_b(z) = \frac{1}{(z + 1)^\alpha},
\]
both satisfy Assumption~\ref{assumptionK}. The gCQ  approximations take the form
\begin{align*}
\left[ K_a(\partial_t^\Delta) f \right]_n = \int_{0}^{\infty} G_a(x) y_n^a(x)\, dx, \quad
\left[ K_b(\partial_t^\Delta) f \right]_n = \int_{0}^{\infty} G_b(x) y_n^b(x)\, dx,
\end{align*}
where
\begin{equation*}
G_a(x) = \frac{\sin(\pi \alpha)}{\pi}  \frac{x^\alpha}{x^{2\alpha} + 2x^\alpha \cos(\pi \alpha) + 1}, \quad G_b(x) = \frac{\sin(\pi \alpha)}{\pi}  x^{-\alpha}, \quad 0 < \alpha < 1,
\end{equation*}
and
\[
y_n^a(x) = y_n(x), \quad y_n^b(x) = y_n(x + 1),
\]
with \( y_n(x) \) given in \eqref{RK_sol}. 

Notice that the function $G_a$ does not fit the behavior for which the quadrature in \cite{BanLo19} was designed. Thus, we use trapezoidal quadrature to evaluate the  history part of the convolution \( \left[ K_a(\partial_t^\Delta) f \right]_n \). Notice that the substitution \( x = e^{\mu/\alpha} \) transforms the integral \eqref{S_hisint}, with \( G(x) = G_a(x) \), into
\begin{align*}
\Sv_n^{\mathrm{his}} = \frac{1}{\alpha} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{\mu/\alpha} G_a(e^{\mu/\alpha}) \left( \prod_{l=n-n_0+1}^{n} \left( \Rv(-\tau_l e^{\mu/\alpha}) \ev_s^\top \right) \right) \Qv^{\mathrm{his}}_{n-n_0}(e^{\mu/\alpha}) \, d\mu.
\end{align*}
Applying the trapezoidal rule yields the approximation  
\begin{align*}
\Sv_n^{\mathrm{his}} \approx \frac{h}{\alpha} \sum_{l=-\widetilde{M}}^{\widetilde{N}} e^{l h/\alpha} G_a(e^{l h/\alpha}) \left( \prod_{l=n-n_0+1}^{n} \left( \Rv(-\tau_l e^{l h/\alpha}) \ev_s^\top \right) \right) \Qv^{\mathrm{his}}_{n-n_0}(e^{l h/\alpha}),
\end{align*}
where the quadrature parameters \( h = 3/40 \) and \( \widetilde{M} = \widetilde{N} = 400 \) are chosen to ensure that the quadrature error is negligible. The maximum absolute error for \( K(z) = K_a(z) \) is computed in the same manner as in \eqref{MaxAbsErr}. The exact solution is given by  
\[
(K_a(\partial_t)f)(t) = \Gamma(\beta + 1) t^{\alpha + \beta} E_{\alpha, \alpha + \beta + 1}(-t^\alpha),
\]
with the Mittag-Leffler function evaluated using the routine from \cite{Gar15}.



The results in Figure~\ref{fig:conv_kaoptGam} show that the two-stage Radau IIA based gCQ  method achieves a full convergence order of 3 on the graded mesh \eqref{gmesh} with \(\gamma = 3/(\alpha + \beta)\).  In Figure~\ref{fig:conv_kadiffGam}, it is evident that the convergence rate on the graded mesh is \(\min\left\{3, \gamma (\alpha + \beta)\right\}\).  Additionally, Figure~\ref{fig:conv_kadiffBetLob} presents the case of \(p > q + 1 + \alpha\), verifying that the  Runge--Kutta  based gCQ method can attain a convergence order of \(\min\{p, q + 1 + \alpha\}\) and maintain this rate for nonsmooth \(f(t)\) on a graded mesh \eqref{gmesh} with \(\gamma = \min\{p, q + 1 + \alpha\}/(\alpha + \beta)\). These results are consistent with  Theorems~\ref{thm:conv_gCQrkGenM} and ~\ref{thm:conv_gCQrkGrad}.

