Proof of Lemma 6
\begin{proof}

Consider the function $\phi(x)=e^{x}R(-x)$. By utilizing \eqref{Rz_prop}, we obtain
\[\phi(0)=R(0)=1,\quad \phi'(0)=-R(0)-R'(0)=0, \]
which further implies
\begin{equation*}
\phi(x)=1+O(x^2)\quad \mbox{as} \quad x\to 0,
\end{equation*}
resulting in the existence of a sufficiently small $r_0 > 0$ and a constant $c_\phi > 0$ such that
\begin{equation*}
|\phi(x)|\le 1+c_\phi x^2\le \e^{c_\phi x^2} \quad \mbox{for} \quad  x\le r_0.
\end{equation*}
Consequently, for $x\le r_0$, where $r_0$ is sufficiently small, we can deduce
\begin{align*}
|R(-x)|&\le \e^{- x}\e^{c_\phi x^2}\le  e^{-x +c_\phi x^2}\le  e^{-(1-c_\phi x) x}\\
&\le \frac{1}{1+(1-c_\phi r_0) x}=  \frac{1}{1+\hat{b}_0 x},
\end{align*}
where
\[\hat{b}_0=1-c_\phi r_0>0.\]
Furthermore, since $R(\infty) = 0$, there exist $r_\infty > r_0$ and $\hat{b}_\infty > 0$ such that
\begin{equation*}
|R(-x)|\le \frac{1}{1+\hat{b}_\infty x} \quad \mbox{for} \quad x>r_\infty.
\end{equation*}
And on the closed interval $x\in[r_0,  r_\infty]$,  we have
\begin{equation*}
\max\limits_{\{r_0\le x\le r_\infty\}}|R(-x)|=:\Upsilon<1.
\end{equation*}
Hence, we can choose a constant $\hat{b}_c>0$ such that
\begin{equation*}
\Upsilon\le \frac{1}{1+\hat{b}_cr_\infty},
\end{equation*}
which leads to
\begin{equation*}
|R(-x)|\le \frac{1}{1+\hat{b}_cx},\quad r_0\le x\le r_\infty.
\end{equation*}
By combining  the aforementioned results, we can derive
\begin{equation*}
|R(-x)|\le \frac{1}{1+\hat{b}x}, \quad x>0,
\end{equation*}
where $\hat{b}=\min\{\hat{b}_0,\hat{b}_c,\hat{b}_\infty\}$.
The proof is concluded.
\end{proof}

