\begin{abstract}
  The basic (and traditional) \emph{crossing number problem} is to determine the minimum number of crossings in a topological drawing of an input graph in the plane.  In this paper, we develop a unified framework that yields fixed-parameter tractable (FPT) algorithms for many generalized crossing number problems in the plane and on surfaces.

  Our main result takes the following form.  We fix a surface~$\surf$, an integer~$r$, and a map $\kappa$ from the set of topological drawings of graphs in~$\surf$ to the nonnegative integers, essentially describing which types of drawings we accept and how we want to count the crossings in them. Then deciding whether a graph~$G$ has a drawing~$D$ on~$\surf$ with $\kappa(D)\le r$ can be done in time quadratic in the size of~$G$.  More generally, we may take as input an edge-colored graph, and distinguish crossings by the colors of the involved edges; and we may allow to perform a bounded number of edge removals and vertex splits to~$G$ before drawing it.  The proof is a reduction to the embeddability of a graph on a two-dimensional simplicial complex, which admits an FPT algorithm by a result of Colin de Verdi\`ere and Magnard [ESA 2021].

  As a direct consequence, we obtain, in a unified way, FPT algorithms for many established topological crossing number variants, including, e.g., fixed-rotation and color-constrained ones.  While some of these variants already had previously published FPT algorithms, for many of them we obtain algorithms with a better runtime.  Moreover, our framework applies to drawings in any fixed surface and, for instance, solves the natural surface generalizations of $k$-planar, $k$-quasi-planar, min-$k$-planar, and $k$-gap crossing numbers, as well as the joint and the edge crossing numbers, the skewness, and the splitting number in surfaces, all of which except the splitting number have not been handled in surfaces other than the plane yet.
\end{abstract}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Introduction}

The \emph{crossing number} problem, minimizing the number of pairwise edge crossings in topological drawings of an input graph,%
\footnote{We defer the formal definitions to \Cref{sec:prelim}.} %
is a long-standing and central task in graph drawing and visualization that comes in many established flavors; see the extensive dynamic survey by Schaefer~\cite{DBLP:journals/combinatorics/SchaeferDS21}.
In this paper, we give a naturally described unified framework that provides efficient algorithms for many of the variants of crossing numbers when parameterized by the solution value (i.e., the respective crossing number).

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%% 
\subparagraph{Many variations on the crossing number problem.}

Recently, we have seen an ``explosion'' of variants of the crossing number problem. One motivation for such variations is that, in practical drawing applications, not every crossing or crossing pattern of edges may be ``equal'' to other crossings.  
For instance, one may want to avoid mutual crossings of the important edges.  Or, to allow crossings only within specific parts of a graph, and not between unrelated parts.  Or, to exclude any crossings of edges of some type.   Or, to allow only certain ``comprehensible'' crossing patterns in order to help with visualizing the graph.  
This is the primary focus of the recent research direction called ``beyond planarity''~\cite{DBLP:journals/csur/DidimoLM19}.

Schaefer~\cite[Chapter~2]{DBLP:journals/combinatorics/SchaeferDS21} characterizes the variants (or flavors) of a crossing number by the drawing style, the method of counting, and the mode of representation. While, in our context, we always use the standard representation of vertices by points and of edges by arcs, there is a variety of admissible drawing styles. For instance, one may restrict the shape of arcs used to draw edges, or restrict the number of crossings on one edge or in a local pattern of crossing edges, the number of edges sharing one crossing, or prescribe or forbid certain local topological properties and patterns of the drawing (also depending on the types or colors of edges). One may also consider drawings in surfaces instead of the traditional plane.
In this context, the \emph{crossing number} of a drawing style (flavor) $\Sigma$ of a graph~$G$ is defined as the minimum count of crossings over all drawings of $G$ which are of the style~$\Sigma$. The `count of crossings' most often means the number of crossing points of pairs of edges, but other methods are conceivable, e.g., counting the pairs of edges which mutually cross an odd number of times.  See below for some concrete examples.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subparagraph{Existing algorithms.}

As far as algorithms are concerned, one usually considers the decision version of the (traditional) crossing number problem: Given an input graph~$G$ with at most $n$ vertices and edges, and an integer~$r$, does $G$ have crossing number at most~$r$?  This problem is \NP-hard, as already proved by Garey and Johnson in 1983~\cite{GareyJ83}, and even in very restrictive settings such as cubic graphs~\cite{Hlineny06}, bounded pathwidth graphs~\cite{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2406-18933}, and planar graphs augmented with a single edge~\cite{DBLP:journals/siamcomp/CabelloM13}.  Moreover, the problem is~\APX-hard~\cite{Cabello13}, and the best known approximation algorithm, by Chuzhoy and Tan~\cite{DBLP:conf/stoc/ChuzhoyT22}, gives an approximation factor that is subpolynomial in the size of the input graph when the graph has bounded degree.

On the other hand, a series of papers were devoted to algorithms that are fixed-parameter tractable (\FPT) in~$r$ (equivalently, in which the runtime has the form $f(r)\cdot\poly(n)$, where $f$ is a computable function of the \emph{parameter}~$r$).
Grohe~\cite{DBLP:journals/jcss/Grohe04} described such an algorithm with a quadratic dependence in~$n$; Kawarabayashi and Reed, in an extended abstract~\cite{DBLP:conf/stoc/KawarabayashiR07}, announced a linear-time dependence in~$n$.
Thus, in theory, we can efficiently compute the exact crossing number of any graph if the crossing number is bounded. However, these two algorithms rely on Courcelle's theorem about the efficient solvability of properties formulated in MSO logic on graphs of bounded treewidth, which results in hard-to-describe algorithms with a dependence in the parameter $r$ that is an exponential tower of height at least four in the case of \cite{DBLP:journals/jcss/Grohe04} and three in the case of \cite{DBLP:conf/stoc/KawarabayashiR07} (this dependence is not stated explicitly in either of the cited papers, but we refer to an analysis in~\cite{Cr-SODA25}).

Two recent approaches avoid resorting to Courcelle's theorem.  First, Colin de Verdi\`ere, Magnard, and Mohar~\cite{cmm-egtds-22} have given a simple linear-time reduction from the crossing number problem to the embeddability of the input graph in a topological space homeomorphic to a certain two-dimensional simplicial complex (2-complex for short), whose complexity depends only on~$r$.  On the other hand, Colin de Verdi\`ere and Magnard~\cite{cm-faeg2-24} have shown that the embeddability of a graph of size~$n$ in a 2-complex of size~$C$ can be done in $2^{\poly(C)}\cdot n^2$.  Put together, these two results imply a $2^{\poly(r)}\cdot n^2$-time algorithm for the crossing number problem.  Even more recently, Lokshtanov et al.~\cite{Cr-SODA25} have given a $2^{\ca O(r\log r)}\cdot n$-time algorithm for the crossing number problem, by reduction to bounded treewidth and dynamic programming, all in time linear in~$n$.

On the other hand, for other flavors of crossing numbers, which are also \NP-hard, the literature is scarce; besides Pelsmajer, Schaefer, and \v{S}tefankovi\v{c}~\cite{DBLP:journals/dcg/PelsmajerSS08} giving \FPT\ algorithms for the odd and pair crossing numbers (via an adaptation of Grohe's algorithm), we are only aware of two very recent papers bringing a general approach to multiple crossing number flavors, which we now detail.

First, M\"unch and Rutter \cite{patterncrossing24} have extended Grohe's approach \cite{DBLP:journals/jcss/Grohe04} to provide a framework for \FPT\ algorithms for computing the exact crossing number of several types of beyond-planar drawings of graphs in the plane, which can be characterized by forbidden combinatorial crossing patterns. The cases include the exact crossing numbers of $k$-planar, $k$-quasi-planar, min-$k$-planar, fan-crossing, and fan-crossing free drawings of a graph for any constant~$k$.  
(Precise definitions on the various flavors of crossing numbers will be given in \Cref{sec:consequences}.)
However, these algorithms also rely on Courcelle's theorem, making the aforementioned drawbacks, in particular the huge dependence in~$r$, unavoidable. M\"unch and Rutter~\cite{patterncrossing24} do not explicitly specify the runtime, but applying Courcelle's algorithm requires a preprocessing step reducing the treewidth, which is only rarely doable in subquadratic time in this context.

Second, Hamm, Klute, and Parada~\cite{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2412-13092}, in a very recent preprint, have announced a result widely generalizing the framework of M\"unch and Rutter \cite{patterncrossing24}, allowing to handle also forbidden topological crossing patterns in drawing types in the plane and, more importantly, bringing the possibility to handle predrawn parts of the input graph (as was done before for the traditional crossing number~\cite{DBLP:conf/compgeom/HammH22}).  However, they again rely on Courcelle's theorem, and their dependence in the size~$n$ of the input graph is an unspecified~polynomial.

Apart from that, we are only aware of sporadic results that apply to very specific problem variants, which Schaefer's survey~\cite{DBLP:journals/combinatorics/SchaeferDS21} treats as borderline cases (of crossing numbers), but we include them here for the sake of generality.
Kawarabayashi and Reed~\cite{DBLP:conf/stoc/KawarabayashiR07} briefly mention the possibility to solve the skewness of a graph in linear \FPT\ time, and N{\"{o}}llenburg et al.~\cite{DBLP:conf/gd/NollenburgSTVWW22} give a non-uniform \FPT\ solution to the splitting number in surfaces.  (The latter is the only algorithm in our list that applies to surfaces other than the plane).


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subparagraph{Our contributions.}

We develop a general framework for \FPT\ algorithms for many crossing number variants.  Our general result takes the following form.  We fix a surface~$\surf$, an integer~$r$, and a map $\kappa$ from the set of (possibly edge-colored) topological drawings of graphs to the nonnegative integers, describing (essentially) which drawings are admissible and how we want to count the crossings of an admissible drawing.  (This map~$\kappa$ has to satisfy a few natural conditions, which hold in particular if $\kappa(D)$ is the number of crossings of~$D$ and the chosen drawing style is ``well-behaved''.)  
Then, deciding whether a graph~$G$ has a drawing~$D$ on~$\surf$ with $\kappa(D)\le r$ can be done in quadratic time in the size~$n$ of~$G$, and exponential in a polynomial of the other parameters, namely: the number of connected components, the genus, and the number of boundary components of~$\surf$; the integer~$r$; and the maximum size of a ``fully crossing'' drawing $D$ satisfying $\kappa(D)\le r$.
This remains true if one is also allowed to perform a bounded number of edge removals (possibly color-restricted) and vertex splits to~$G$ before drawing it (these numbers being also parameters).

Applying this result, we obtain quite directly \FPT\ algorithms with runtime quadratic in the size~$n$ of the input graph, and exponential in a polynomial of the other parameters, for many established crossing number variants, which we now list, without claiming exhaustiveness.  
First, this concerns the crossing number problem when restricted to many established drawing styles, such as $k$-planar, $k$-quasi-planar, min-$k$-planar, fan-crossing, weakly and strongly fan-planar, $k$-gap, and fixed-rotation drawings.  This is new for the $k$-gap and fixed-rotation cases, and for the other cases, for any fixed $k$, we improve the \FPT\ runtime over the aforementioned works \cite{patterncrossing24} and \cite{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2412-13092}.
Second, we may assign colors to the edges of the input graph and count the crossings differently depending on the colors of the edges involved, capturing, e.g., the joint crossing number on surfaces and its generalizations.  
Third, we may (assuming suitable additional properties) handle problems that do not count the crossings, but rather the number of edges, or pairs of edges, involved in crossings, such as the edge, pair, and odd crossing numbers.  And fourth, before drawing our graph, we may allow a bounded number of modifications of our graph, namely edge removals and vertex splits; this includes, e.g., the skewness and the splitting numbers.

Moreover, all these four aspects of crossing numbers can be combined arbitrarily, and our main results are valid not only in the plane but for arbitrary surfaces.  This shows the versatility of our unified approach.

The proof of our main theorem is a reduction to the problem of deciding embeddability of a graph on a two-dimensional simplicial complex already mentioned~\cite{cm-faeg2-24}.  Again, for some crossing number flavors, \FPT\ algorithms in the planar case were known, and almost all of them relied on Courcelle's algorithm, leading to a huge multi-level exponential dependence in the parameters, and/or had an unspecified polynomial dependence in the size~$n$ of the input graph.  In contrast, our results, while applying also to surfaces, do not depend on Courcelle's algorithm and are only singly-exponential in a polynomial of the parameters.  

Finally, we remark that, with this approach, the extension from the planar case to the surface case essentially comes for free (assuming the considered crossing number flavor behaves well in the surface).  While this extension is natural and worth studying, two benefits are to be emphasized.  First, the framework also applies to non-orientable surfaces, homeomorphic to a sphere with a number of ``crosscaps''.  Placing $k$~crosscaps in the plane amounts to choosing $k$ specific points in the plane that can be traversed by an arbitrary number of pairwise crossing edges of the planar drawing ``for free'', without accounting for the induced crossings at these points.  Second, we allow surfaces with boundary, which is needed for some subtle variations of the fan-planar crossing number.


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subparagraph{Organization of the paper.}

After the preliminaries (\Cref{sec:prelim}), we describe in detail our general framework and state our main result (\Cref{sec:metasection}), which is then proved (\Cref{sec:proofmeta}).  We then list the numerous applications to crossing number variants (\Cref{sec:consequences}).  
\onlyapx{Due to space restrictions, we defer proofs of some statements and some less important technical considerations to the Appendix. We mark statements with deferred proofs~by~~\apxmark.}


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Preliminaries}\label{sec:prelim}

\subparagrapho{Graphs and surfaces.}

In this paper, graphs are finite and undirected, but not necessarily simple unless specifically noted. The \emph{size} of a graph $G$ is the number of vertices plus the number of edges of~$G$.  A \emph{vertex split} of a graph~$G$ at vertex~$v$ creates a new vertex~$v'$ and replaces some of the edges incident to~$v$ by making them incident to~$v'$ instead of~$v$.

We assume some basic knowledge of topology, in particular surface topology; see, e.g., Armstrong~\cite{a-bt-83} or Mohar and Thomassen~\cite{mt-gs-01}, but we recall the most important notions.  A \emph{surface}~$\surf$ is a topological space obtained from finitely many disjoint solid, two-dimensional triangles by identifying some of their edges in pairs.  Surfaces are not necessarily connected.  By the classification theorem, up to homeomorphism, each \emph{connected} surface~$\surf$ is specified by whether it is orientable or not, by its \emph{(Euler) genus} (for orientable surfaces, it is twice the number of ``handles'', and for non-orientable ones, it is the number of ``crosscaps''), and by its number of boundary components.  In this paper, by `genus' we always mean the Euler genus; moreover, the genus of a disconnected surface is the sum of the genuses of its connected components.  The plane is not a surface according to our definition, but in this paper it can always be substituted with a disk, which is a surface.  A \emph{self-homeomorphism} of~$\surf$ is just a homeomorphism from~$\surf$ to~$\surf$.

\subparagraph{(Topological) drawings of graphs.}

In this paper, any drawing of any graph in any topological space maps distinct vertices to distinct points, and has finitely many intersection points, each avoiding the images of the vertices; however, an intersection point may involve two or more pieces of edges.  In more formal terms, and introducing more terminology, the previous sentence means the following.  A \emph{curve}~$c$ in a topological space~$\cal X$ is a continuous map from the closed interval $[0,1]$ into~$\cal X$.  The \emph{relative interior} of~$c$ is the image, under~$c$, of the open interval~$(0,1)$.  In a {\em drawing}~$D$ of a graph $G$ in a topological space~$\cal X$, vertices are represented by points of~$\cal X$ and edges by curves in~$\cal X$ such that the \emph{endpoints} $c(0)$ and~$c(1)$ of a curve~$c$ are the images of the endpoints of the corresponding edge.  Moreover, we assume that distinct vertices are mapped to distinct points and that the relative interiors of the edges avoid the images of the vertices.  Given a point $x\in\cal X$, its \emph{multiplicity} in~$D$ is the number of pairs $(c,t)$ such that $c(t)=x$, where $c$ is a curve representing an edge of~$G$ in~$D$, and $t\in(0,1)$.  An \emph{intersection point} (shortly an \emph{intersection}) of~$D$ is a point with multiplicity at least two.  We only consider drawings~$D$ with finitely many intersection points.

For drawings on graphs on surfaces, we put additional constraints: We only consider drawings of graphs that contain no isolated vertex, and whose image avoids the boundary of the surface.  To remind the reader of all these assumptions, we sometimes say that drawings on surfaces are \emph{decent}.  A point of multiplicity two corresponds to a \emph{crossing}, if no local perturbation of the curves can remove this intersection, or to a \emph{tangency}, otherwise.  Each edge~$e$ of a drawing~$D$ is subdivided into \emph{pieces} by the intersection points along~$e$.

Most of these restrictions are benign in a graph drawing context;  for example, drawings can be perturbed to avoid the boundary of the surface, and isolated vertices are irrelevant.  Decent drawings are more general than \emph{good drawings}~\cite{cd98262b-e086-3d8d-b726-cd66f05da520}, namely, decent drawings in which every intersection point has multiplicity two and is a crossing (not a tangency), as commonly assumed in the graph drawing world. Moreover, a good drawing~$D$ of a graph~$G$ is called \emph{simple} if $G$ is simple, no edge self-crosses, no two adjacent edges cross, and no two edges cross more than once.

The (traditional) \emph{crossing number} of a graph $G$ in a surface $\surf$ is the least number of crossings over all good drawings of $G$ in~$\surf$.  Inspired by the discussion in Schaefer \cite[Chapter~2]{DBLP:journals/combinatorics/SchaeferDS21}, in this paper we take a very general view of crossing numbers, which we describe in \Cref{sec:metasection}, and we also refer to \Cref{sec:consequences} for the definitions of many other flavors of crossing numbers that fall in our framework.





\subparagraph{2-complexes.}

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \def\svgwidth{.6\linewidth}
    \input{figures/2complex.pdf_tex}
    \caption{(a): An example of a two-dimensional simplicial complex, or 2-complex for short. (b): An embedding of some graph with four vertices and three edges on the same 2-complex.}
    \label{fig:2complex}
\end{figure}

In this paper, a \emph{$2$-complex} (or two-dimensional simplicial complex) is a topological space obtained from a simple graph (without loops or multiple edges) by attaching a solid, two-dimensional triangle to some of its cycles of length three; see \Cref{fig:2complex}.%
\footnote{Our definition of 2-complex slightly departs from the standard one; it is the same as a geometric simplicial complex of dimension at most two, realized in some ambient space of dimension large enough.} %
The class of (topological spaces represented by) 2-complexes is rather general; it contains all graphs, all surfaces, all $k$-books (although in this paper, we only need 2-complexes in which every edge is incident to at most two triangles), and any space obtained from a surface by identifying finitely many finite subsets of points and by adding finitely many edges between any two points.  We emphasize that we mostly regard a 2-complex as a data structure for representing its underlying topological space; nontrivial 2-complexes admit many other homeomorphic 2-complexes, since a subdivided graph is homeomorphic to its original, and a surface has several non-isomorphic triangulations; the choice of the 2-complex plays a role only in the complexity analysis of our algorithms.

Since a 2-complex is a topological space, we have already defined the notion of drawing of a graph~$G$ into a 2-complex~$\complex$.  In particular, vertices of~$G$ may lie anywhere on~$\complex$, and edges of~$G$ as curves may traverse several edges and triangles of~$\complex$.  (Recall that decent drawings were defined for surfaces only.)
An \emph{embedding} of~$G$ into~$\complex$ is a drawing without intersection point.
A key property is that if $\complex$ and~$\complex'$ are homeomorphic 2-complexes, then $G$ embeds in~$\complex$ if and only if it embeds in~$\complex'$.  (Actually, this property is valid not only for 2-complexes, but for any topological space.)

We can easily represent 2-complexes algorithmically, for example, by storing the vertices, edges, and triangles, and the incidences between them.

\subparagraph{Embeddability of graphs on 2-complexes. }

The \emph{embeddability problem} takes as input a graph~$G$ and a 2-complex~$\complex$, and the task is to decide whether $G$ has an embedding in~$\complex$.  Our algorithms will be reductions to the embeddability problem, which is fixed-parameter tractable in the size of the input 2-complex:

\begin{theorem}[{Colin de Verdière and Magnard~\cite[arXiv version, Theorem~1.1]{cm-faeg2-24}%
\footnote{The conference proceedings version of~\cite{cm-faeg2-24} gives a slightly worse bound on the running time, cubic in~$n$.  The theorems that we state take into account the improvement in the latest arXiv version.}}]
\label{thm:embedding}
  One can solve the embeddability problem (of graphs in 2-complexes) in $2^{\poly(C)}\cdot n^2$ time, where $C$ is the number of simplices of the input $2$-complex and $n$ is the number of the vertices and edges of the input graph.
\end{theorem}
(For graphs of bounded branchwidth, the computation can be done in time linear in~$n$~\cite[Theorem~1.2]{cm-faeg2-24}.)  In case of positive instances, a representation of the corresponding embedding can be computed; we defer this discussion to \Cref{sec:compute}.

\subparagraph{Storing drawings on surfaces.}\label{sec:storing}

We need to represent drawings of graphs on a surface~$\surf$ combinatorially.  Actually, we only care about drawings up to self-homeomorphisms of~$\surf$.  In this paper, we represent drawings on surfaces as subsets of the vertex-edge graph of some triangulation of~$\surf$.  \onlynoapx{(An alternative possibility would be through \emph{extended combinatorial maps}~\cite[Section~2.2]{cm-tgis-14}, but this appears to be too complicated for our purposes.)}

Specifically, a \emph{triangulation} is a graph~$T$ embedded on~$\surf$ such that each face of~$T$ (each connected component of the complement of the image of~$T$) is homeomorphic to a disk bounded by three edges, and all three incident vertices and edges are pairwise distinct.  In particular, a triangulation is a 2-complex.  Let $T'$ be the graph made of the vertices and edges of~$T$ that lie in the interior of~$\surf$.  Let $W=(w_1,\ldots,w_k)$ be a set of $k$~walks in~$T'$, such that each edge of~$T'$ is used at most once by the union of these walks.  This set~$W$ naturally gives rise to a decent drawing~$D$ of a graph~$G$ on~$\surf$: The graph~$G$ has $k$~edges, each drawn as $w_1,\ldots,w_k$ on~$\surf$; the vertices of~$G$ are the at most~$2k$ endpoints of the walks in~$W$.  We say that the pair $(T,W)$ is a \emph{representation} of~$D$.  

A representation of a colored drawing is a representation $(T,W)$ of the corresponding uncolored drawing, together with the data of a color for each walk in~$W$.  Its \emph{size} is the size of a data structure to represent it, which is $\ca O(tc)$, where $t$ is the number of triangles and $c$ is the number of colors.
\onlyapx{Conversely, every (decent) drawing~$D$ on~$\surf$ can be represented by such a pair~$(T,W)$; see Lemma~\ref{lem:represent-drawings}.}

\onlynoapx{Conversely (as proved in Lemma~\ref{lem:represent-drawings}), every (decent) drawing~$D$ on~$\surf$ can be represented by such a pair~$(T,W)$ in which $T$ is made of $\ca O(a+g+b+u)$ triangles, where $a$, $g$ and~$b$ are the number of connected components, the genus, and the number of boundary components of~$\surf$, and the intersection points of~$D$ subdivide the edges of~$D$ into $u$ pieces in total.}

