\section{The framework: description and results}\label{sec:metasection}

In the following, we fix a surface~$\surf$ with $a$ connected components, genus~$g$, and $b$ boundary components.  We consider \emph{colored graphs}, in which each \emph{edge} is labeled with a positive integer.  A \emph{colored drawing} in~$\surf$ is a (decent) drawing of a colored graph~$G$ in~$\surf$ in which each edge of the drawing inherits the color of the corresponding edge of~$G$.  Let $\cfD$ be either the set of (decent) colored drawings of graphs on~$\surf$, or the set of (decent) colored drawings of \emph{simple} graphs on~$\surf$, depending on the context.

Our general result uses the following definition, in which $\kappa$ should be thought as a generalization of the (traditional) crossing number:
\begin{definition}[Crossing-counting pair]\label{def:crossing-counting-pair}
Let $\kappa:\cfD\to\Z_+\cup\{\infty\}$ be a map from~$\cfD$ to the set of non-negative integers (or infinity), and $\lambda:\Z_+\to\Z_+$ be a non-decreasing function.
We say that $(\kappa,\lambda)$ is a \emph{crossing-counting pair} if:
\begin{enumerate}
\item for any two drawings $D$ and~$D'$, we have $\kappa(D')\le\kappa(D)$ whenever $D'$ is obtained from~$D$ by one of the following operations:
  \begin{enumerate}[(a)]
    \item the removal of a vertex or an edge;
    \item the addition of a vertex whose image is disjoint from the image of~$D$ (and from the boundary of~$\surf$);
    \item the addition of an edge, of an arbitrary color, connecting two vertices in~$D$, and otherwise disjoint from the image of~$D$ (and from the boundary of~$\surf$);
    \item a self-homeomorphism of~$\surf$;
  \end{enumerate}
  \item for any drawing~$D^\times\in\cfD$ such that each edge carries at least one intersection in~$D^\times$ (either a self-intersection or an intersection with another edge --- common endpoints do not count) and $\kappa(D^\times)<\infty$, the number of pieces of~$D^\times$ is at most~$\lambda(\kappa(D^\times))$;
  \item one can compute $\lambda(i)$ in $2^{\ca O(i)}$ time. 
    \end{enumerate}
\end{definition}
Note that \Cref{def:crossing-counting-pair} implies that $\kappa$ is unaffected by any of the operations (b), (c), and~(d), since such operations can be reversed by an operation of type (a) or~(d).

As a special but important case, if $\kappa(D)$ denotes the number of crossings in~$D$, or is~$\infty$ if $D$ is not a good drawing (i.e., has an intersection of multiplicity at least three or a tangency intersection), then $\kappa$ satisfies the conditions of \Cref{def:crossing-counting-pair} with $\lambda(i)=4i$, and $\kappa(D)$ is the number of crossings in~$D$.  This motivates the term `{crossing-counting pair}'.

We now introduce the following problem:
\ProbIII{\DCN~(for the class~$\cfD$ of drawings, in surface~$\surf$)}%
{A colored graph~$G$ with colors in~$\{1,\ldots,c\}$, with at most $n$ vertices and edges, and a non-negative integer~$r$.}%
{Is there a colored drawing $D$ of~$G$ in~$\surf$ such that $\kappa(D)\le r$?}

Assuming $\kappa$ is computable, we prove that the \DCN{} problem is fixed-parameter tractable in the topology of the surface, the number~$c$ of colors, and the value of~$r$:
\begin{theorem}\label{thm:main}
Let $\surf$ be a surface with $a$ components, genus~$g$, and $b$ boundary components, $\cfD$ the set of colored drawings of graphs in~$\surf$  (possibly restricted to simple graphs), and $(\kappa,\lambda)$ a crossing-counting pair.  

Let $\delta$ be a non-decreasing function such that, given a representation of size~$s$ of a (decent, colored) drawing~$D$, one can in time $\delta(s)$ compute $\kappa(D)$.

Then the \DCN{} problem, on the input instance $(G,r)$, can be solved in time $2^{\poly(a+g+b+c+\lambda(r))}\cdot\delta(\ca O(a+g+b+\lambda(r)))\cdot n^2$, where $n$ is the size of~$G$.
\end{theorem}

Let us stress that in all our applications, the factor $\delta(\cdot)$ will be at most exponential, and will play no role in the runtime of the algorithm.

More generally, motivated by the splitting number and the skewness parameters (cf.~\Cref{sub:delete-split}), we consider the following problem, in which we additionally allow a prescribed number of vertex splits and edge removals (for each color) before drawing:
\ProbIII{\MDCN~(for the class~$\cfD$ of drawings, in~$\surf$)}%
{A colored graph~$G$ with colors in~$\{1,\ldots,c\}$, with at most $n$ vertices and edges, a non-negative integer~$r$, and non-negative integers $p$~and $q_1,q_2,\ldots,q_c$.}%
{Is there a colored drawing~$D'$ in~$\surf$, with $\kappa(D')\le r$, of a colored graph~$G'$, such that $G'$ is obtained from~$G$ by performing at most $p$ successive vertex splits and by removing, for $i=1,\ldots,c$, at most $q_i$ edges of color~$i$?}

The following generalization of \Cref{thm:main} states that, assuming $\kappa$ is computable, the \MDCN{} problem is fixed-parameter tractable in the topology of the surface, the number~$c$ of colors, the number $p+\sum_iq_i$ of allowed vertex splits and edge removals, and the value of~$r$:
\begin{theorem}\label{thm:mainmod}
Let $\surf$ be a surface with $a$ components, genus~$g$, and $b$ boundary components, $\cfD$ the set of colored drawings of graphs in~$\surf$ (possibly restricted to simple graphs), and $(\kappa,\lambda)$ a crossing-counting pair.  

Let $\delta$ be a non-decreasing function such that, given a representation of size~$s$ of a (decent, colored) drawing~$D$, one can in time $\delta(s)$ compute $\kappa(D)$.

Then the \MDCN{} problem, on the input instance $(G,r,$ $p,q_1,\ldots,q_c)$, can be solved in time $2^{\poly(a+g+b+c+p+q+\lambda(r))}\cdot\delta(\ca O(a+g+b+\lambda(r)))\cdot n^2$, where $n$ is the size of~$G$ and $q=\sum_iq_i$.
\end{theorem}

The above theorems reduce to the embeddability problem and apply \Cref{thm:embedding}.  Our algorithms are quadratic in~$n$, but this is solely due to the fact that the algorithm for \Cref{thm:embedding} is quadratic in~$n$; our reductions are linear in~$n$ (and exponential in a polynomial in the other parameters).

Moreover, for positive instances, we can compute an actual representation of the corresponding drawing:
\begin{theorem}\apxmark\label{thm:explicit}
  For every positive instance of the \MDCN{} problem, we can compute a representation of the corresponding drawing, of size the size $n$ of the input graph times a polynomial in $a+b+g+c+p+q+\lambda(r)$, without overhead in the runtime compared to \Cref{thm:mainmod}.
\end{theorem}

