\section{Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:mainmod}}\label{sec:proofmeta}

Since Theorem~\ref{thm:main} is a special case of Theorem~\ref{thm:mainmod}, we only provide a proof for the latter.  We fix a surface~$\surf$ with $a$ connected components, genus~$g$, and $b$ boundary components, and a crossing-counting pair~$(\kappa,\lambda)$ for drawings on~$\surf$.  We denote by $\cfD$ the set of (decent) drawings of graphs on~$\surf$, possibly~restricted~to~simple~graphs.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Preliminary definitions}

We need some specific terms to refer to parts of a 2-complex~$\complex$.  An \emph{isolated edge} of~$\complex$ is an edge incident to no triangle.  The \emph{surface part} of~$\complex$ is the union of all its triangles, together with the vertices and edges incident to at least one of them.  A \emph{singular point} of~$\complex$ is a point that has no open neighborhood homeomorphic to an open disk, a closed half-disk, or an open segment.

Let $D$ be a drawing of a graph~$G$ on~$\surf$.  We need to define what we mean by \emph{cutting}~$\surf$ along~$D$ (see Figure~\ref{fig:reduc-complex}(a--b)).  Intuitively, we cut $\surf$ along the relative interior of edges of~$D$, resulting in a topological space that is actually homeomorphic to a 2-complex.  More formally, recall that $D$ is represented by a pair $(T,W)$ where $T$ is a triangulation of~$\surf$ and $W$ is a set of walks in the triangulation and in the interior of the surface, such that each edge of~$T$ is used at most once.  Cutting $\surf$ along~$D$ is best described by starting from the collection of all unidentified triangles of~$T$ and doing the following:
\begin{itemize}
    \item whenever two directed edges $e$ and~$e'$ of triangles are identified to a single edge in~$T$, and that edge is not used by any walk in~$W$, we identify $e$ and~$e'$.  Note that this process automatically identifies the source endpoints of~$e$ and~$e'$, and similarly for their target endpoints;
    \item whenever two vertices of the resulting triangulation correspond to the same vertex of~$T$, and that vertex is an endpoint of at least one walk in~$W$, we identify these two vertices.
\end{itemize}
Since we only perform a subset of the identifications of vertices and edges in~$T$, the resulting space is naturally a 2-complex.

