\subsection{Description of the reduction}

In this section, we describe the reduction for the proof of \Cref{thm:mainmod}.  We actually keep a high-level view, leaving some algorithmic considerations aside.  In particular, we view 2-complexes as topological spaces, without worrying about their decomposition into simplices.  \onlynoapx{We shall prove the validity of the reduction in \Cref{sec:correctness-meta}, and provide the actual algorithmic details in \Cref{sec:end-meta}.}

Consider an instance $(G,r,p,q_1,\ldots,q_c)$ of the \MDCN{} problem.  Of course, if $\cfD$ is restricted to drawings of simple graphs, then we assume that $G$ is simple.  In this section, we define an uncolored graph $G_2=G_2(G,r,p,q_1,\ldots,q_c)$ and a set of 2-complexes $\Gamma=\Gamma(r,p,q_1,\ldots,q_c)$ such that our \MDCN{} instance is positive if and only if $G_2$ embeds in at least one of the 2-complexes in~$\Gamma$.

We first present informally the main ideas for the reduction; see Figures \ref{fig:reduc-graph} and~\ref{fig:reduc-complex}.  Assume that a graph~$G'$, obtained from~$G$ by edge removals and vertex splits, has a drawing~$D'$ in~$\surf$.  Let $D^\times$ be the subdrawing of the subgraph of~$G'$ made of the edges that are involved in an intersection in~$D'$; the definition of a counting-crossing pair implies that we can guess~$D^\times$.  We cut~$\surf$ along~$D^\times$ and add isolated edges connecting the endpoints of the edges in~$D^\times$, obtaining a 2-complex~$\complex_2$ in which $G'$~embeds.  If $G'$ is allowed to result from~$G$ by edge additions and vertex splits, we modify the 2-complex appropriately, by guessing which points of the 2-complex must be identified and where the endpoints of the isolated edges must be inserted.  We obtain a complex~$\complex_4$ in which $G$~embeds.  There remains to encode the colors of the edges, in order to make sure that the reduction is valid.  For this purpose, we turn each edge of~$G$ and each isolated edge of~$\complex_4$ into a \emph{necklace} encoding the allowed colors, such that a necklace of a certain type in the 2-complex can only be used by a necklace of the same type in~$G$.  This results in an (uncolored) graph~$G_2$ embedded in a complex~$\complex_5$.

As just mentioned, one step of our reduction is to combinatorially encode the colors of edges of~$G$ in a larger uncolored graph.  For this purpose, we define a \emph{necklace} of length three, thickness~$h$, and beads of size~$k$ to be the graph obtained from a path of length three by (1) replacing each edge with $h$ parallel edges, and then (2) attaching $k$ loops to each of the two vertices that were not the endpoints of the path.  The intuition is that a necklace of length three does not embed in a necklace of smaller thickness or with smaller beads, so replacing the edges of the graph of color~$i$ with necklaces of length three, color $i+1$, and beads of size $c-i+1$, and creating necklaces of the same type in the 2-complex, will prevent graph necklaces to use 2-complex necklaces of different types.  (We actually use slightly adjusted values for the thickness and the size of the beads.)

\begin{figure}
    \def\svgwidth{\linewidth}
    \input{figures/reduc-graph.pdf_tex}
    \caption{The construction of the graph~$G_2$.  (a): The graph~$G$; the red edges have color~1 and the blue edge has color~2.  (b): The graph~$G_1$.  The new edges, in black, have color~3.  (c): The graph~$G_2$ in the case $c=2$, $\lambda(r)=p=q=1$.  Each edge of color~$i$ is replaced with a necklace of length three, thickness $p+2q+2\lambda(r)+i=5+i$, and beads of size $c-i+2=4-i$.  On the right,  a close-up on the replacement for a black edge in~$G_1$, which is a necklace of length three, thickness eight, and beads of size one.}
    \label{fig:reduc-graph}
\end{figure}

In more detail, we first describe the uncolored graph~$G_2=G_2(G,r,p,q_1,\ldots,q_c)$, which will be the common input to all embeddability instances.  See Figure~\ref{fig:reduc-graph}.
\begin{itemize}
    \item Let $G_1$ be the colored graph obtained from~$G$ by attaching a 4-clique to each vertex~$v$ of~$G$, that is, by adding three new vertices making a 4-clique with~$v$. The edges of each attached 4-clique get color $c+1$.  (This is a new color, and we assume $q_{c+1}=0$.)
    \item Let $G_2$ be the uncolored graph obtained from~$G_1$ by replacing each edge of color~$i$ with a necklace of length three, thickness~$p+2q+2\lambda(r)+i$, where $q=\sum_jq_j$, and beads of size~$c-i+2$.
\end{itemize}

\begin{figure}%\vspace*{-3ex}
    \def\svgwidth{\linewidth}
    \input{figures/reduc-complex.pdf_tex}
    \caption{The construction of a 2-complex~$\complex_5$.  (a): The surface~$\surf$ and the drawing~$D^\times$.  (b): The 2-complex~$\complex_1$ is obtained by cutting~$\surf$ along~$D^\times$.  Note that each vertex of~$G$ (in particular, the one in the bottom of the image) on~$\surf$ corresponds to a single point in~$\complex_1$, while, in this particular example, each intersection point in~$D$ of multiplicity~$i$ corresponds to~$4i$ points in~$\complex_1$.  (c) The 2-complex~$\complex_2$ is obtained by adding isolated edges corresponding to the edges of~$D^\times$; we define the color of each isolated edge to be that of the corresponding edge in~$D^\times$.  (d) The 2-complex~$\complex_3$ is obtained by adding $q_i$ isolated edges of color~$i$ for each color~$i$.  Here color~1 is represented in red, color~2 is represented in blue, and $q_1=1$, $q_2=0$. (e) The 2-complex~$\complex_4$ is obtained by identifying two points of~$\complex_3$ (here $p=1$).  (f) The 2-complex $\complex_5$ is obtained by replacing each isolated edge of color~$i$ with a necklace of length three, thickness $p+2q+2\lambda(r)+i$, and beads of size $c-i+2$.  The necklaces are represented in a figurative way.
    }
    \label{fig:reduc-complex}
\end{figure}

Let us now describe our set~$\Gamma$ of 2-complexes in which we try to embed~$G_2$.  See Figure~\ref{fig:reduc-complex}.  These 2-complexes depend on several successive choices.  First, let~$D^\times\in\cf D$ be a colored drawing on~$\surf$ such that $\kappa(D^\times)\le r$ and such that each edge carries at least one intersection in the drawing.
\begin{itemize}
    \item Let $\complex_1$ be obtained from~$\surf$ by cutting~$\surf$ along the relative interior of each edge of~$D^\times$.
    \item Let $\complex_2$ be obtained from~$\complex_1$ by doing the following: For each edge of~$D^\times$ of color~$i$, connecting points $x$ and~$y$ of~$\complex_1$, we add an isolated edge connecting $x$ and~$y$ to the 2-complex, which is said to have \emph{color}~$i$.  (This may require subdividing~$\complex_1$ to turn $x$ and~$y$ into vertices.)
\end{itemize}

Let $U$ be the set of points of~$\complex_2$ that correspond to a single point of the interior of~$\surf$.  In detail, $U$ is made of (1) the points of~$\complex_2$ that correspond to an endpoint of an edge in~$D^\times$, and (2) the interior of the surface part of~$\complex_2$.

For each $i=1,\ldots,c$, let $(x_i^1,y_i^1),\ldots,(x_i^{q_i},y_i^{q_i})$ be pairs of points on~$U$.  (These points are not necessarily distinct.  There are finitely many choices up to a self-homeomorphism of~$\surf$.)
\begin{itemize}
    \item Let $\complex_3$ be obtained from~$\complex_2$ by doing the following: For each $i=1,\ldots,c$ and each $j=1,\ldots,q_i$, we add to the 2-complex an isolated edge connecting $x_i^j$ and~$y_i^j$, which is said to have \emph{color}~$i$.
\end{itemize}
For each unordered partition $p=p_1+\ldots+p_k$ of the integer~$p$, where $p_1,\ldots,p_k$ are positive integers, and for each $i=1,\ldots,k$, let $z_i^1,\ldots,z_i^{p_i+1}$ be $p_i+1$ points on $U$ (which is also a part of~$\complex_3$), such that all these $p+k$ points are pairwise distinct.  (Note that the points $z_i^j$ are not necessarily distinct from the points $x_{i'}^{j'}$ and $y_{i'}^{j'}$.)
\begin{itemize}
    \item Let $\complex_4$ be obtained from~$\complex_3$ by doing the following: For each $i=1,\ldots,k$, we identify the points $z_i^1,\ldots,z_i^{p_i+1}$ to a single point~$z_i$.
    \item Let $\complex_5$ be obtained from~$\complex_4$ by replacing each isolated edge~$e$ of~$\complex_1$ of color~$i$ with a necklace of length three, thickness~$p+2q+2\lambda(r)+i$, and beads of size $c-i+2$.
\end{itemize}
To conclude, $\complex_5$ depends on the choice of the drawing~$D^\times\in\cfD$, of the points $x_i^j$, $y_i^j$, of the partition of~$p$, and of the points~$z_i^j$.  We let $\Gamma=\Gamma(r,p,q_1,\ldots,q_c)$ to be the set of 2-complexes~$\complex_5$ that can be obtained in this way, over all these choices.

