\section{Applications: FPT algorithms for diverse crossing number variants}\label{sec:consequences}

In this section, we demonstrate the wide applicability of our framework (\Cref{sec:metasection}) to diverse flavors of crossing number problems under various counting methods and topological restrictions of the admissible drawings.  We first give some general comments.  In this section, $\surf$ denotes a surface and, unless noted otherwise, $\cfD$ denotes the set of drawings of (possibly non-simple) graphs on~$\surf$.  For all problems except for the strongly fan-planar crossing number problem, the boundary components are irrelevant, so we could assume no boundary without losing generality.  We reduce to the \DCN{} problem except for the problems in \Cref{sub:delete-split}, for which we need to reduce to the more general \MDCN{} problem.  In nearly all cases, only good drawings of graphs are considered.  In all cases, for positive instances, we can compute a representation of the corresponding drawing, by a straightforward application of \Cref{thm:explicit}.



%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Variations on the traditional crossing number}
\begin{toappendix}
  \onlyapx{\subsection{Variations on the traditional crossing number}}
\end{toappendix}

\subparagrapho{$k$-planar drawings.}
We recall the well-studied notion of \emph{$k$-planar} drawings introduced by Pach and T\'oth~\cite{DBLP:journals/combinatorica/PachT97}; these are {good} drawings in the plane such that every edge is involved in at most $k$ crossings. For any drawing~$D$, we define $\kappa(D)$ to be the number of crossings in~$D$, if $D$ is a good $k$-planar drawing, and $\kappa(D)=\infty$ otherwise. The \emph{$k$-planar crossing number}, for any positive integer constant~$k$, is then the smallest value of $\kappa(D)$ over all good drawings $D$ of~$G$. Note that a graph may have no $k$-planar drawing at all, and then its $k$-planar crossing number is undefined, or~$\infty$ in our definition.
Already for $k=1$, deciding the existence of a $1$-planar drawing is \NP-hard even for very restricted inputs \cite{DBLP:journals/algorithmica/GrigorievB07,DBLP:journals/siamcomp/CabelloM13}.
Furthermore, one may naturally consider this definition in an arbitrary surface $\surf$, which we call the \emph{$k$-surface crossing number} in~$\surf$ (due to the lack of an established terminology).

\subparagraph{$k$-quasi-planar and min-$k$-planar drawings.}
There are similar concepts of \emph{$k$-quasi-planar}~\cite{DBLP:journals/combinatorica/AgarwalAPPS97} (no $k$ edges pairwise cross) and of \emph{min-$k$-planar}~\cite{DBLP:conf/gd/BinucciBBDDHKLMT23} (for every two edges with a common crossing, one carries at most $k$ crossings) good drawings in the plane, which give rise to the corresponding crossing number flavors in the plane as before, and we may likewise generalize them to the \emph{$k$-quasi-surface} and \emph{min-$k$-surface} \emph{crossing numbers} in a surface~$\surf$.
Though, one should also note that many authors consider variants of the $k$-planar, $k$-quasi-planar, and min-$k$-planar drawings for simple graphs only and additionally require the admissible drawings to be simple.  These \emph{simple-drawing variants} can be very different from the non-simple ones already on simple graphs; see, e.g., \cite[Chapter~7]{schaefer2017crossing} and \cite{DBLP:conf/gd/HlinenyK24}, but they also smoothly fit into our framework.

\subparagraph{$k$-gap drawings.}
Another known concept is that of \emph{$k$-gap crossing number} \cite{DBLP:journals/tcs/BaeBCEE0HKMRT18,DBLP:journals/jgaa/BeusekomPS22}, minimizing the number of crossings over $k$-gap drawings in the plane, where a \emph{$k$-gap} drawing of a graph~$G$ is a good drawing $D$ of $G$ admitting a mapping from each crossing in $D$ to one of the two involved edges such that at most $k$ crossings are mapped to each edge of~$G$. This problem is \NP-hard for $k=1$~\cite{DBLP:journals/tcs/BaeBCEE0HKMRT18}. Again, the $k$-gap crossing number is naturally generalized to any surface~$\surf$.

\subparagraph{Applying our framework.}
Given an integer $r\geq0$, each of the problems to decide whether a graph $G$ has $k$-planar ($k$-quasi-planar, or min-$k$-planar) crossing number at most $r$ in the plane is fixed-parameter tractable in~$r$; see the recent paper by M\"unch and Rutter \cite{patterncrossing24} and the preprint by Hamm, Klute, and Parada~\cite{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2412-13092}.
The parameterized complexity of these three problems in other surfaces, and of the $k$-gap crossing problem altogether, has not, to our knowledge, been studied prior to us. Even in the known planar cases, we improve the runtime of aforementioned works~\cite{patterncrossing24,DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2412-13092} from an unspecified \FPT\ time down to explicit~$2^{\poly(r)}\cdot n^2$:
\begin{proposition}\label{prop:kplanar-etc}%\apxmark
Let $k\geq1$ and $r\geq0$ be integers, $\surf$ a surface with $a$ connected components, genus~$g$, and $b$ boundary components, and let $\Pi\in\{$`$k$-surface', `$k$-quasi-surface', `min-$k$-surface', `$k$-gap'$\}$. The problems to decide whether an input graph $G$ of size $n$ has $\Pi$-crossing number at most~$r$ in $\surf$ can be solved in time $2^{\poly(a+g+b+r)}\cdot n^2$.
%
The same conclusion holds for the simple-drawing $\Pi$-crossing number variants.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
  The proof relies on \Cref{thm:main} with a single color ($c=1$).  We have to give an appropriate crossing-counting pair $(\kappa,\lambda)$ for each of the listed properties~$\Pi$, such that $\kappa$ is efficiently computable, and verify the requirements of \Cref{def:crossing-counting-pair}.  Let $\kappa=\kappa_\Pi$ map a drawing~$D$ to $\infty$ if it is not a good drawing or if it violates the property~$\Pi$, and to the number of crossings of~$D$ otherwise.  In particular, if $k$ is greater than the number of crossings in $D$, then $D$ always satisfies~$\Pi$.

  Given a drawing $D$, one may in time polynomial in the size of a representation of $D$ verify that $D$ is a good drawing satisfying $\Pi$, and so to compute $\kappa_\Pi(D)$. This is an easy routine in all cases of $\Pi$ except when $\Pi=$\,`$k$-gap'; in the latter case we additionally employ a standard network flow algorithm to decide the existence of a mapping from the crossings to their incident edges respecting ``capacities'' of edges to accept at most $k$ crossings each.

  Since we only consider good drawings, $\lambda(i)=4i$ satisfies the assumption of \Cref{def:crossing-counting-pair}. Likewise, it is easy to see that $\kappa_\Pi$ is non-increasing under the operations listed in \Cref{def:crossing-counting-pair}.
  Hence, our conclusion follows by an application of \Cref{thm:main} to $(\kappa_\Pi,\lambda)$ and the graph $G$.

  %

  We now consider the simple-drawing $\Pi$-crossing number variant.  For this case, we need to restrict the set $\cfD$ to drawings of \emph{simple} graphs on~$\surf$.  (Otherwise, the function $\kappa_\Pi$ could jump up to $\infty$ by adding a parallel uncrossed edge, which would intersect twice its twin edge and thus make the drawing non-simple.)  The rest of the argument is identical.
\end{proof}

\subparagraph{Fan-crossing and fan-planar drawings.}
We briefly mention another topic recently introduced in the graph drawing area \cite{DBLP:journals/combinatorics/0001U22}. A good drawing is \emph{fan-crossing} if all edges crossing the same edge are incident to a common vertex. M\"unch and Rutter \cite{patterncrossing24} provide an \FPT\ algorithm for minimizing the number of crossings over fan-crossing drawings.
Restricting further, a fan-crossing drawing is called \emph{weakly fan-planar} if, whenever edges $f_1$ and $f_2$ with a common end $v$ both cross an edge $e$, the (sub)arcs of both $f_1,f_2$ from $v$ to the crossing with $e$ lie on the same side of~$e$; and it is called \emph{strongly fan-planar} if, moreover, in the described situation of $f_1,f_2$ both crossing $e$, the union $e\cup f_1\cup f_2$ in the drawing does not enclose both ends of~$e$ (in the plane).
We refer to Cheong et al.~\cite{DBLP:conf/gd/CheongFKPS23} for a closer discussion, and to \Cref{fig:fan} for an illustration.
\begin{figure}%\vspace*{-2ex}
    \centering
    \def\svgwidth{.6\linewidth}
    \input{figures/fan.pdf_tex}
    \caption{(a) A strongly fan-planar drawing.  (b) A weakly fan-planar drawing that is not strongly fan-planar.  (c) A fan-crossing drawing that is not weakly fan-planar.}
    \label{fig:fan}
\end{figure}

It is interesting to observe that the property of strong fan-planarity is \emph{not invariant} under self-homeo\-morphisms of the sphere (though it is invariant under self-homeomorphisms of the plane, or equivalently of a large disk encompassing the whole drawing).  In particular, the drawings in \Cref{fig:fan}(a) and (b) are homeomorphic when seen on the sphere.


The \emph{weakly (strongly) fan-planar crossing number} is the minimum number of crossings over all weakly (strongly) fan-planar drawings in the plane. While M\"unch and Rutter \cite{patterncrossing24} leave the question of the parameterized tractability of fan-planar crossing numbers open, Hamm, Klute, and Parada~\cite{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2412-13092} provide an \FPT\ algorithm for the weakly fan-planar crossing number.  Our framework is capable of including all three variants, namely, also the strongly fan-planar crossing number, because our framework allows for surfaces with boundary, and thus can capture the ``infinite face'' of the drawing:
\eric{stating a formal proposition}

\begin{proposition2rep}\label{prop:fan}\apxmark
Let $r\geq0$ be an integer. The problem to decide whether an input graph $G$ of size $n$ has a strongly fan-planar, weakly fan-planar, or fan-crossing drawing with at most~$r$ crossings in the plane can be solved in time $2^{\poly(r)}\cdot n^2$.
\end{proposition2rep}
\begin{proof}
  The proof is very similar to that of \Cref{prop:kplanar-etc}, specialized to the plane.  We only need to be able to recognize strongly fan-planar, weakly fan-planar, or fan-crossing drawings in polynomial time, which is not difficult.
  \end{proof}
\onlyapx{The proof is very similar to that of \Cref{prop:kplanar-etc}.} Extensions to arbitrary surfaces are obviously possible.




\subparagraph{Drawings which may not be good.}
To illustrate the applicability of our framework with crossing number variants that do not necessarily use good drawings, we introduce the following example. For an integer $k\geq2$, we define the \emph{$k$-intersecting crossing number} of a graph $G$ to be the minimum value $\ell$ such that there is a decent drawing of $G$ with $\ell$ intersection points in which the multiplicity of every intersection point is at most~$k$.
Observe that, for every graph $G$, the traditional crossing number of $G$ is at most $k\choose2$ times its $k$-intersecting crossing number, and this bound can be tight.

The following is again a straightforward consequence of our framework:
\begin{proposition2rep}\label{prop:k-intersecting}\apxmark
Let $k\geq2$ and $r\geq0$ be integers, $\surf$ a surface with $a$ connected components, genus~$g$, and $b$ boundary components. The problem to decide whether an input graph $G$ of size $n$ has $k$-intersecting crossing number at most~$r$ in $\surf$ can be solved in time $2^{\poly(a+g+b+k+r)}\cdot n^2$.
\end{proposition2rep}
\begin{proof}
  We apply \Cref{thm:main} in a setup very similar to the proof of \Cref{prop:kplanar-etc}; we only need a different definition of the crossing-counting pair $(\kappa,\lambda)$.
  Let $\kappa(D)=\kappa_k(D)$ be equal to $\infty$ if $D$ has an intersection point of multiplicity greater than $k$, and to the number of intersection points of $D$ otherwise. Setting $\lambda(i)=\lambda_k(i)=2ki$, the pair $(\kappa_k,\lambda_k)$ easily fulfills \Cref{def:crossing-counting-pair}.
  Hence, our conclusion again follows by an application of \Cref{thm:main} to $(\kappa_k,\lambda_k)$ and the graph $G$.
\end{proof}
Note that in this problem it does matter that we allow tangential intersections of edges in our framework (however, if a restricted version of $k$-intersecting crossing number which disallows tangential intersections was considered, our framework would handle it smoothly as well.).

We refer to \Cref{sub:nontraditional} for more examples of different methods of counting crossings.

