\subsection{Crossing problems with colored edges}
\begin{toappendix}
  \onlyapx{\subsection{Crossing problems with colored edges}}
\end{toappendix}

We now turn our attention to problems in which the colors of intersecting edges play a role.

\subparagraph{Joint crossing number and generalizations.} As an illustration, we consider the joint crossing number problem in a surface, introduced by Negami~\cite{DBLP:journals/jgt/Negami01}.
Consider two disjoint graphs $G_1$ and $G_2$, both embeddable in a surface $\surf$. The \emph{joint crossing number} of $G_1$ and $G_2$ in~$\surf$ is the minimum number of crossings over all good drawings $D$ of the union $G_1\cup G_2$ in $\surf$, such that $D$ restricted to each one of $G_1$ and $G_2$ is crossing-free. In other words, we may color the edges of $G_1$ red and those of $G_2$ blue, and then admit only red--blue crossings in the drawings of $G_1\cup G_2$.
This problem is \NP-hard for any fixed genus $g\geq6$ by Hlin\v{e}n\'y and Salazar~\cite{DBLP:conf/isaac/HlinenyS15}, and the complexity of this problem parameterized by the solution value has not been \mbox{studied prior to our work}.

It actually appears natural to introduce the following new generalized problem, which more directly relates to and is obviously at least as hard as the traditional crossing number problem:
\ProbIII{\cccn{}~(in surface~$\surf$)}%
{A colored graph~$G$ with colors in~$\{1,\ldots,c\}$, and a symmetric matrix~$M$ of size $c\times c$ with nonnegative integer values.}%
{Is there a good colored drawing $D$ of~$G$ in~$\surf$ such that, for each pair $i,j\in\{1,\ldots,c\}$, the number of crossings involving two edges, one colored $i$ and the other~$j$, is at most~$M_{i,j}$?}

\begin{proposition2rep}\label{prop:CCCN}\apxmark
Let $\surf$ be a surface of with $a$ connected components, genus~$g$, and $b$ boundary components. The \cccn{} in $\surf$ of the input graph $G$ of size $n$ and matrix $M$ can be solved in time $2^{\poly(a+g+b+r)}\cdot n^2$, where $r=\sum_{1\leq i\leq j\leq c}M_{i,j}$.

Consequently, one can decide whether the joint crossing number of graphs $G_1$ and $G_2$, each embeddable in~$\surf$, is at most~$r$ in time $2^{\poly(a+g+b+r)}\cdot n_0^2$, where $n_0$ is the size of~$G_1\cup G_2$.
\end{proposition2rep}
\begin{proof}
  Without loss of generality, we assume that $c\le r+1$, since merging the colors corresponding to empty rows of~$M$ will not change the problem.  Again, as in the proof of \Cref{prop:kplanar-etc}, we have to define an appropriate crossing-counting pair $(\kappa_M,\lambda)$.  Let $\kappa_M(D)$ be equal (straightforwardly) to the number of crossings in a good drawing~$D$, except that $\kappa_M(D)=\infty$ if $D$ is not good, or if for some pair $i,j\in\{1,\ldots,c\}$, the number of crossings involving two edges, one colored $i$ and the other~$j$, is larger than~$M_{i,j}$. This is easily computable in polynomial time in the representation of~$D$.  Moreover, $\kappa_M$ is non-increasing under the operations listed in \Cref{def:crossing-counting-pair}.  Letting $\lambda(i)=4i$, \Cref{thm:main} clearly applies to $(\kappa_M,\lambda)$ and the graph~$G$, and yields the result.

  In the case of the joint crossing number problem, for $i=1,2$, we use color~$i$ for~$G_i$, define $G$ to be the disjoint union of $G_1$ and~$G_2$, let $M=\begin{pmatrix}0&r\\r&0\end{pmatrix}$, and apply the previous result.
\end{proof}
%
We remark that \cite{DBLP:conf/isaac/HlinenyS15} also considers a modified variant called homeomorphic joint crossing number of graphs $G_1$ and $G_2$ in which $G_1$ and $G_2$ are given as cellularly embedded in $\surf$, and the task is to minimize the number of crossings of $G_1\cup G_2$ under an additional condition that the subdrawings of each of $G_1$ and $G_2$ are homeomorphic to the given embeddings. Our algorithm can also be applied to this variant via a reduction deferred to the Appendix.
\begin{toappendix}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subparagrapho{Homeomorphic joint crossing number.}

In the related {\em homeomorphic joint crossing number} problem in an orientable surface~$\surf$ without boundary, the input graphs $G_1$ and $G_2$ are cellularly embedded in $\surf$ (every face is homeomorphic to a disk) and the solution must be composed of embeddings of $G_1$ and $G_2$ that are each homeomorphic to the input one (the rotation systems must be the same, possibly up to reversal).  Given an integer~$r\ge0$, the goal is to determine whether there is a way to achieve this with at most $r$ crossings between $G_1$ and~$G_2$.  The problem is also NP-hard if the genus of~$\surf$ is at least six~\cite{DBLP:conf/isaac/HlinenyS15}.  We prove:

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:jointcr}
Let $\surf$ be an orientable surface of with $a$ connected components, genus~$g$, and without boundary, and an integer~$r\ge0$. The \emph{homeomorphic joint crossing number}  in $\surf$ of the input graphs $G_1$ and~$G_2$ can be solved in time $2^{\poly(a+g+r)}\cdot n_0^2$, where $n_0$ is the size of~$G_1\cup G_2$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.5\linewidth]{hardening}
    \caption{The hardening of a graph (fixing the rotation system in an orientable surface).}
    \label{fig:hardening}
\end{figure}

The strategy is to reduce the problem to the regular joint crossing number.  First, for a graph $H$ cellularly embedded in an orientable surface $\surf$, let the \emph{hardening} of $H$ be the graph $\bar H$ constructed as follows (\Cref{fig:hardening}):
\begin{itemize}
    \item For each edge $e=uv\in E(H)$, we replace $e$ with two new internally disjoint paths $P_e$ and $Q_e$ of length three, called the \emph{twin paths of $e$}, with the new vertices denoted in such a way that $P_e=\big(u,w^1_{u,e},w^2_{v,e},v\big)$ and $Q_e=\big(u,w^2_{u,e},w^1_{v,e},v\big)$.
    \item For each vertex $v\in V(H)$ with the cyclic order of adjacent edges $(e_1,e_2,\ldots,e_p)$ in the given embedding in $\surf$, we add the edges of a cycle (the \emph{hardening cycle} of $v$) on $\big(w^1_{v,e_1},w^2_{v,e_1}, w^1_{v,e_2},w^2_{v,e_2}, \ldots, w^1_{v,e_p},w^2_{v,e_p}\big)$ in this cyclic order.
\end{itemize}
In relation to the operation of hardening, we prove the following technical lemma:
\begin{lemma}\label{lem:4to1}
    Let $H^2$ be the graph constructed from a graph $H$ by replacing each edge with a pair of parallel edges (called a \emph{twin edge pair}).
    If $D$ is a drawing of $H^2$ in a surface $\surf$ with at most $4k$ crossings (but no self-crossings), then there is a subdrawing $D'\subseteq D$ isomorphic to $H$, obtained by selecting one edge out of each twin edge pair of $H^2$, with at most $k$ crossings.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    We choose $D'\subseteq D$ by selecting one out of each twin edge pair of $H^2$ independently at random. The probability that a crossing $x$ of $D$ is a crossing in $D'$ is $\frac12\cdot\frac12=\frac14$ (both edges of $x$ are selected), and so the expected number of crossings in $D'$ is $\frac14\cdot4k=k$ and there has to be a choice of $D'$ with at most $k$ crossings.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:hardening}
    Let $G_1$ and $G_2$ be two graphs cellularly embedded in an orientable surface~$\surf$, and let $\bar G_1$ and $\bar G_2$ denote their hardenings.
    The homeomorphic joint crossing number of $G_1$ and $G_2$ in $\surf$ is at most $k$ if and only if the joint crossing number of $\bar G_1$ and $\bar G_2$ in $\surf$ is at most $4k$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    Let $G:=G_1\dot\cup\,G_2$.
    The `$\Rightarrow$' direction is easy.
    Assuming $D$ is a drawing of $G$ with at most~$k$ crossings solving the homeomorphic joint crossing number problem of $G_1$ and $G_2$, one may draw the twin paths of each edge of $G$ closely along this edge in $D$ (and each old crossing hence generates $4$ new crossings), and the hardening cycles of all vertices of $G$ can be drawn there without crossings thanks to having homeomorphic subdrawings of $G_1$ and $G_2$ in $D$.

    In the `$\Leftarrow$' direction, let $\bar D$ be a drawing witnessing a solution to the joint crossing number problem of $\bar G_1$ and $\bar G_2$ with at most $4k$ crossings.  We can clearly assume that no edge self-crosses in~$\bar D$.
    Restricting to only the subdrawing of $\bar D$ formed by the twin paths of all edges of $G$ and suppressing degre-$2$ vertices, we may apply \Cref{lem:4to1} and conclude that there is a subdrawing $D'\subseteq\bar D$ with at most $k$ crossings which is isomorphic to (a subdivision of) $G$.
    It remains to verify that, for $i=1,2$, the subdrawing of~$D'$ representing $G_i$ (which is an embedding by the definition of the joint crossing number problem) is homeomorphic to the given embedding of $G_i$.

    It is well known that two cellular embeddings of the same graph in an orientable surface are homeomorphic (mirror image of the whole embedding allowed) if and only if their rotation systems are the same.
    For the embedding of $G_i$ represented by $D'$, this is ensured by embeddings of the hardening cycles of $\bar G_i$ within the solution $\bar D$. This finishes the proof.
\end{proof}
\end{toappendix}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subparagraph{Fixing the rotation system.}
\begin{toappendix}
  \onlyapx{\subparagraph{Fixing the rotation system.}}
\end{toappendix}

In a similar spirit but more generally, a natural restriction on crossing number problems is to fix the clockwise cyclic order of edges around each vertex (the \emph{rotation system}).  The traditional crossing number with rotation system remains \NP-hard~\cite{DBLP:journals/algorithmica/PelsmajerSS11}, and the question of its parameterized complexity (with respect to the number of crossings) has not been explicitly considered so far.

With our framework, we provide an efficient solution to the variant of the \cccn{} where the input additionally prescribes the cyclic permutation of the edges around each vertex of~$G$, and give a solution in the plane.

\begin{toappendix}
Formally, we introduce the following problem:
\ProbIII{\cccn{}~with Rotation System}%
{A colored graph~$G$ with colors in~$\{1,\ldots,c\}$, a symmetric matrix~$M$ of size $c\times c$ with nonnegative integer values, and a system of cyclic permutations $\big(\pi_v:v\in V(G)\big)$ where the domain of $\pi_v$ is the set of edges incident to $v$ in~$G$.}%
{Is there a good colored drawing $D$ of~$G$ in the \emph{plane} such that;\begin{itemize}\parskip0pt\item for each pair $i,j\in\{1,\ldots,c\}$, the number of crossings in $D$ involving two edges, one colored $i$ and the other~$j$, is at most~$M_{i,j}$, and\item for each vertex $v\in V(G)$, the clockwise rotation of edges around $v$ in $D$ is~$\pi_v$?\end{itemize}\vspace*{-2ex}}
\end{toappendix}

\begin{theorem2rep}\label{prop:CCCNrot}\apxmark
The \cccn{}~\textsc{with Rotation System} (in the plane) of the input connected graph $G$ of size $n$, matrix $M$, and rotation system $\big(\pi_v:v\in V(G)\big)$ can be solved in time $2^{\poly(r)}\!\cdot n^2$, where $r=\sum_{1\leq i\leq j\leq c}M_{i,j}$.
\end{theorem2rep}
\onlyapx{The proof, not very difficult but quite technical, is deferred to the appendix; it uses an ``encoding'' of the rotations and (more importantly) of their orientations in the drawings by gadgets sketched in \Cref{fig:CCCNrot} to reduce to the ordinary \cccn{}.}

We remark that the problem naturally generalizes to orientable surfaces, but the proof is more complicated, so in this version we only formulate and solve it in the plane.

\begin{figure}%\vspace*{-3ex}
    \centering
    \def\svgwidth{\linewidth}
    \input{figures/CCCNrot.pdf_tex}
    \caption{The construction of the graph~$G_1$ for the proof of \Cref{prop:CCCNrot}, in the case $r=1$.  (a) The graph~$G$, with the spanning tree~$T$ in thick lines, shown with the desired rotation system.  (b) The graph~$G_1$; its original edges are black, and the new colors red, blue and green are as pictured.  Red edges cannot carry any crossing, and blue-blue and green-green crossings are forbidden.  (c) A valid drawing of the graph~$G_1$ with crossings.}
    \label{fig:CCCNrot}
\end{figure}

\begin{proof}
  We are going to reduce this problem to the ordinary \cccn{} (with $c+3$ colors and a suitable matrix $M'$), and then apply \Cref{prop:CCCN}.

  For our reduction, we introduce three new colors which we call red, green, and blue. Recall that a \emph{wheel} of $k$ spokes is the graph obtained from the cycle $C_k$ by adding a new vertex adjacent to all vertices of the cycle (called a \emph{rim}). We modify the given colored graph $G$ to a colored (including the three new colors) graph $G_1$ in the following steps; see \Cref{fig:CCCNrot}:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item We choose any spanning tree $T\subseteq G$ (notice that $G$ is assumed connected).
    \item For every vertex $v\in V(G)$ we create a new wheel $W_v$ of $k_v$ spokes where $k_v=\degr_G(v)+3\degr_T(v)$, assign the color red to all edges of~$W_v$, and fix an orientation of the rim cycle---deemed \emph{clockwise}.  For each edge~$e$ incident with~$v$, we choose a set~$R_v^e$ of consecutive vertices on the rim cycle of~$W_v$, made of four vertices if $e\in T$ and of a single vertex otherwise, in such a way that these sets $R_v^e$ are pairwise disjoint and the clockwise cyclic order of the terminals on $W_v$ agrees with the rotation $\pi_v$.  These wheels will be used to ``fix the rotation'' of edges incident to $v$ up to mirror image.
    \item For every edge $e\in E(G)$ we create a new isolated edge $e^o$, called an \emph{original edge}, which inherits the color of $e$ from~$G$.
    \item For every edge $e\in E(T)$ we create a new graph $N_e$, called a \emph{$3$-chain}, as follows. Start with $4r-1$ copies of the wheel of $6$ spokes, with all edges red and denoted by $N_e^1,\ldots,N_e^{4r-1}$. Then, for $i=0,1,\ldots,4r-1$, we make three new edges joining three consecutive rim vertices of $N_e^{i}$ (if $i\ge1$, otherwise we leave pendant vertices) to three consecutive rim vertices of $N_e^{i+1}$ (if $i+1\le4r-1$, otherwise we leave pendant vertices) in such a way that $N_e$ is planar. These three edges get color blue if $i$ is even, and green otherwise. The purpose of the $3$-chains will be to ``transfer the orientation'' from one vertex of $G$ to another.
    \item Finally, we construct the graph $G_1$ by identifying vertices from the previous building components. For every $e=uv\in E(G)\setminus E(T)$ we identify one end of the original edge $e^o$ with the unique vertex in $R_e^u$ (of the wheel $W_u$), and the other end with the unique vertex in~$R_e^v$ (of $W_v$).  If $e=uv\in E(T)$, we assign the four vertices of~$R_e^u$, in clockwise order, to one endpoint of~$e^o$ and then to three pendant vertices of one side of~$N_e$, and assign the four vertices of~$R_e^v$, in counterclockwise order, to the other endpoint of~$e^o$ and then to the three pendant vertices of the other side of~$N_e$, in such a way that the resulting graph $W_u\cup N_e\cup W_v$ is planar.
  \end{itemize}

  Then we define a matrix $M'$ which extends $M$ as follows; for $i=\mbox{`red'}$ and any $j$ let $M'_{i,j}=M'_{j,i}=0$ (red edges cannot be crossed), for $i\in\{\mbox{`green',`blue'}\}$ and $j\in\{1,\ldots,c\}$ let $M'_{i,j}=M'_{j,i}=3r$, and for $i=\mbox{`blue'}$ and $j=\mbox{`green'}$ let $M'_{i,i}\!=M'_{j,j}\!=0$ and $M'_{i,j}=M'_{j,i}=9r$.

  We apply \Cref{prop:CCCN} to the \cccn{} in the plane with the input $G_1$ and~$M'$, and prove (in a constructive way) that this modified problem has a solution if and only if the given instance of \cccn{}~\textsc{with Rotation System} with the input $G$, $M$ and $\big(\pi_v:v\in V(G)\big)$ has a solution.

  ($\Leftarrow$) First, assume that a colored drawing $D$ of $G$ is a solution of the latter problem. For every vertex $v$ of $D$ we thus know that the clockwise rotation of edges incident to $v$ agrees with the specified~$\pi_v$. Let $\delta_v$ be a closed disk centered at $v$ in $D$ such that $\delta_v$ is disjoint from all edges non-incident to $v$ in $D$ and from all intersection points of~$D$. We replace, in the drawing $D$, the interior of $\delta_v$ with an embedding of the wheel $W_v$ in $\delta_v$ such that, for each edge~$e$ of~$G$ incident to~$v$, the vertex of $W_v$ that is the end of the original edge $e^o$ of $G_1$ coincides with a point in which the image of $e$ in $D$ intersects the boundary of~$\delta_v$ (this is possible because $D$ respects the cyclic order~$\pi_v$ at~$v$).
  Doing this for all $v\in V(G)$, we turn $D$ into a drawing $D_0$ of the subgraph of $G_1$ formed by the vertex wheels and the original edges, where $D_0$ has precisely the same set of (colored) crossings as~$D$.

  We now turn $D_0$ into a drawing $D_1$ of $G_1$ by adding images of the $3$-chains $N_e$ for every $e\in E(T)$. We draw $N_e$ in a close neighborhood of the original edge $e^o$ in $D_0$ such that the red edges stay uncrossed. For every $f\in E(G)$ such that $f^o$ crosses $e^o$ in $D_0$, we let $f^o$ cross a selected triple of green or blue edges of $N_e$. Moreover, if $f\in E(T)$, we let the same triple of green (or blue) edges of $N_e$ cross a triple of blue (respectively, green) edges of $N_f$. Since $N_e$ contains (alternately) $2r$ green triples and $2r$ blue triples, we can always do the choice such that the mutually crossing triples of $N_e$ and $N_f$ are of the opposite colors among blue and green (even if we have all $r$ crossings as self-crossings of the edge $e^o$).

  Every crossing (of $e^o$ and $f^o$) in $D_0$ thus contributes in $D_1$ at most $3$ crossings of original edges with a blue or a green edge, and at most $3\cdot3=9$ crossings between a green and a blue edge. There is no green-green and no blue-blue crossing. Thus, the obtained drawing $D_1$ of $G_1$ is admissible with respect to the constraint matrix $M'$ defined above.

  ($\Rightarrow$) We now consider a drawing $D'_1$ of $G_1$ in the plane respecting the constraints of~$M'$. In particular, no red edge, and so none of the wheels used in the construction of $G_1$, is crossed. We may without loss of generality assume that each of the wheels $W_v$ for $v\in V(G)$ in $D'_1$ is drawn such that the rest of $D'_1$ is drawn in the face of $W_v$ bounded by the rim cycle; otherwise, we can redraw each of the rim edges closely along the uncrossed spoke edges.

  Let $D'$ be the colored drawing of $G$ obtained from $D'_1$ by removing all $3$-chains and contracting each wheel $W_v$ for $v\in V(G)$ into a single vertex. Then $D'$ immediately satisfies the crossing constraints of~$M$. Furthermore, for every $v\in V(G)$, since $W_v$ is uniquely embeddable in the plane, the clockwise cyclic ordering of the edges around $v$ in $D'$ is either $\pi_v$ or its reversal.  Thus, there only remains to prove that the orientation (clockwise or counterclockwise) of all the wheels in $D'$ is the same.

  \eric{slightly different attempt}To prove this, we remark that the subgraph of a 3-chain made of two consecutive wheels together with the three edges connecting them is 3-connected, and thus has a unique planar embedding.  Since every such subgraph is embedded in~$D'$ (because there are no blue-blue or green-green crossings), we obtain that consecutive wheels in~$D'$ are either both oriented in the same way as in the planar embedding of~$N_e$, or both oriented in the opposite way.  Using this argument $4r$ times implies that two wheels $N_u$ and~$N_v$ are oriented in the same way if $u$ and~$v$ are connected by an edge of~$T$.  The result follows since $T$ is a spanning tree of~$G$.  
\end{proof}


