\section{Conclusions}

\subparagrapho{Summary.}
Obviously, \Cref{thm:mainmod} allows for almost endless possibilities of combining drawing restrictions and methods of counting crossings, analogously to what we have done in \Cref{sec:consequences}.  One can, for instance, combine bounded vertex splitting with (color-constrained) edge removal and counting the (again color-constrained, and possibly with an orthogonal color set) crossings, and all together combined with topological restrictions of admissible drawings.  This shows the versatility of our approach.  Altogether, our general framework encompasses most existing crossing number variations, and even more general ones, implying, in a unified way, fixed-parameter tractable algorithms for these crossing number problems in any surface.

\subparagraph{Limitations.}
There exist, however, some intrinsic limitations in our approach. First, it is capable of handling only crossing number flavors in which the value of $\kappa(D)$ of a drawing $D$ functionally bounds the total number of intersection points in $D$ (at least in some of the optimal instances of the drawing, if not in all). A typical example not fulfilling this requirement is the (para-\NP-hard) \emph{local crossing number} \cite[Section 3.2: Local crossing number]{DBLP:journals/combinatorics/SchaeferDS21}, defined as the smallest $k$ for which the graph admits a $k$-planar drawing. Second, since the value of $\kappa$ is not allowed to grow when deleting vertices and edges from $D$ (by \Cref{def:crossing-counting-pair}), we cannot handle flavors that require certain additional edges or vertices to be present in local crossing patterns. An example of this are the \emph{$k^+$-real face} drawings \cite{DBLP:conf/wg/BinucciBDHKLMT23}, which require every face of a drawing $D$ to be incident to at least $k$ vertices of the graph (in addition to incident crossings), or the maximal $1$-planar drawings.

Another weakness of the approach, in particular compared to Hamm and Hlin\v{e}n\'y~\cite{DBLP:conf/compgeom/HammH22} and to the very recent preprint by Hamm, Klute, and Parada~\cite{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2412-13092}, is our more limited ability to handle predrawn parts of the input graphs, essentially restricted to only fixing uncrossable parts of the graph via rigid subembeddings and to special restrictions like the fixed rotation system. Fixing subdrawings of large parts of the input graph that can be crossed by other edges in the solution currently seems out of reach with our techniques.



\subparagraph{Remarks and possible extensions.}
We now provide some remarks and possible extensions of our framework.  First, we used surfaces with boundary in a non-trivial way only at one place, namely, to study strongly fan-planar drawings.  We believe that boundaries can be useful, though, because they allow us to pinpoint specific parts of the surface.  Actually, in our arguments, we considered drawings up to homeomorphism of the surface~$\surf$, without any special restriction.  As a variation, we could require that our homeomorphisms map each of the $b$ boundary components to itself.  Our arguments carry through; this only affects the running time by a $b!$ factor (see Lemma~\ref{lem:enum}).

A possible extension of the \MMDCN{} problem that we have not considered would be to replace the surface~$\surf$ with an arbitrary 2-complex.  We leave open whether such an extension is possible, and also potential applications of this generalized result.

