% \noindent\textbf{Difference from Other Surveys.} Our work addresses a critical gap in the literature by providing the first survey specifically focused on model extraction attacks and defenses for large language models. While \citet{zhao2025survey} offer a comprehensive analysis of extraction attacks across distributed computing environments, their work covers machine learning models broadly with limited focus on the unique challenges posed by language model architectures. \citet{wang2024unique} and \citet{das2025security} provide extensive coverage of general security and privacy threats to LLMs, but treat model extraction as just one of many security concerns without delving into the technical nuances specific to language model extraction. \citet{liu2025ethical} examine ethical security issues in LLMs with emphasis on societal impacts rather than technical extraction mechanisms. In contrast, our survey offers an in-depth technical examination of extraction vulnerabilities specific to transformer architectures, token-level prediction mechanisms, and contextual processing capabilities that define modern language models. We provide detailed analysis of specialized attack methods like prompt extraction, training data recovery, and knowledge distillation that specifically target language models.


