\section{Syntax of Directed Logic}\label{sec:syntax_of_directed_logic}%
We introduce the syntax of directed first-order logic with a natural deduction-style proof system. The main syntactic judgements for types, terms, formulas and entailments are presented in \Cref{fig:syntax:types_terms,fig:syntax:formulas,fig:syntax:entailments}. As a guiding intuition, the reader can refer to \Cref{fig:semantics_overview} to see how the syntax of directed first-order logic is semantically interpreted in the preorder model.

The types and terms of directed first-order logic are a straightforward axiomatization of simply typed $\lambda$-calculus (e.g., \cite{Pitts1995categorical}) with unit, product, and function types. We leave the judgements for types and terms in \Cref{fig:syntax:types_terms} since they follow exactly those of STLC. We shall blur the distinction between a ``type" (resp., ``term"/``formula") and the derivation tree for the judgement representing it, understanding that a precise definition is given only for the latter. The two notions can be used interchangeably due to a suitable metatheorem on derivation reconstruction and unambiguousness of types (resp., ``term"/``formula") not reported here. For simplicity, we will omit the judgements for equality of formulas (and entailments) induced by equality of terms. As customary in logic, we omit for clarity's sake the metatheoretic operations which apply weakening to formulas in order to place them in the correct context; these will become explicit and play a crucial role in the doctrinal semantics of \Cref{sec:doctrinal_semantics}. We interleave the definition of the judgements with the definition of \emph{theory} \cite{Dalen2013logic}: theories allow the logic to be enriched by adding axioms and ``generating" symbols from a signature, e.g., base types, terms, propositions, and axioms.

\begin{definition}[Signatures and judgements]\label{def:sign_judg} The following series of pairs signature/judgement is given, where we do not explicitly indicate the dependence between a definition and the signatures that come before it; all judgements are inductively defined by the rules in \Cref{fig:syntax:types_terms,fig:syntax:formulas,fig:syntax:entailments}:
   \begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1em]
    \item {[Sign.]} A \emph{type signature} $\Sigma_B$ is defined as a set of symbols $\Sigma_B$, which represents a collection of base types.
    \item {[Judg.]} The set of \emph{type derivations} generated by a type signature $\Sigma_B$, denoted as $\set{A \type}$, is inductively defined by the judgement ``$A \type$" given in \Cref{fig:syntax:types_terms}, along with the judgements for \emph{contexts} $\set{\Gamma \ctx}$, i.e. finite lists of types.
    \item {[Sign.]} A \emph{term signature} $\Sigma_F$ is a set of function symbols $\Sigma_F$ with functions $\dom,\cod : \Sigma_F \> \set{A \type}$.
    \item {[Judg.]} The set of \emph{term derivations} generated by a term signature $\Sigma_F$ is $\set{\Gamma \vdash t : A}$, assuming $A \type$ and $\Gamma \ctx$.
    \item {[Sign.]} A \emph{term equality signature} $\Sigma_E$ is a set $\Sigma_E$ with functions $\mathsf{eqC} : \Sigma_E \> \set{\Gamma \ctx}$, $\mathsf{eqT} : \Sigma_E \> \set{A \type}$, and $\mathsf{eqL}, \mathsf{eqR}\!:\!(e\!:\!\Sigma_E)\!\>\!\set{\mathsf{eqC}(e) \vdash t\!:\!\mathsf{eqT}(e)}$ dependent on $e$.
    \item {[Judg.] The set of \emph{term equality judgements}} generated by $\Sigma_E$ is $\set{\Gamma \vdash t = t' : A}$, assuming $\Gamma \vdash t, t' : A$.
    \item {[Sign.]} A \emph{formula signature} $\Sigma_P$ is a set of predicate symbols $\Sigma_P$ with functions $\mathsf{neg},\mathsf{pos} : \Sigma_P \> \set{A \type}$.
    \item {[Judg.]} The set of \emph{formula derivations} $\set{[\Theta\mid\Delta\mid\Gamma]\ \varphi \prop}$ generated by the formula signature $\Sigma_P$ is described in \Cref{fig:syntax:formulas}, assuming $\Theta,\Delta,\Gamma\ctx$.
    \item {[Judg.]} The set of \emph{propositional contexts} is denoted as $\set{[\Theta\mid\Delta\mid\Gamma]\ \Phi \propctx}$ and given in \Cref{fig:syntax:formulas}. These are simply finite lists of formulas in the same context.
    \item {[Sign.]} \emph{Axioms} are given by a set $\Sigma_A$ and three (dependent) functions ${\mathsf{actx} : \Sigma_A \> \set{\Gamma \ctx}}$, ${\mathsf{hyp}\!:\!(a\!:\!\Sigma_A)} {\>\!\set{[\mathsf{actx}(a)]\ \Phi \propctx}}$, ${\mathsf{conc} : (a : \Sigma_A) \> \set{[\mathsf{actx}(a)]\ \varphi \prop}}$.
    \item {[Judg.]} The set of \emph{entailments} generated by axioms $\Sigma_A$ and the previous signatures is defined as in \Cref{fig:syntax:entailments}.
   \end{itemize}
\end{definition}

We will use the notation $[\Theta,n:N \mid \Delta,d:D \mid \Gamma,p:P]\ \varphi(n,d,d,p)$ to indicate (some of) the free variables of the formula $\varphi$, omitting other variables for brevity. Similarly for terms ${[\Gamma,a:A,b:B]}\ t(a,b) \vdash C$ as in standard presentations.

In the rule $\Rulereindex$ we use a meta-theoretical function on formula derivations $\varphi(n,\n d,d,p)$ which substitutes terms $\eta,\delta,\rho$ for the variables $n,d,p$.

