\begin{definition}[Substitution of terms in formulas]\label{def:substitution_in_formulas}
Given a derivation tree for a formula $[\Theta,n:N \mid \Delta,d:D \mid \Gamma,p:P]\ \varphi(n,\n d,d,p) \prop$, we can substitute any triple of terms
  $\Theta,\Delta \vdash \eta : N$, $\Delta \vdash \delta : D$, $\Gamma,\Delta \vdash \rho : P$ for the variables $n,d,p$ in the $\varphi$. We denote the substituted formula simply as $\varphi(\eta,\delta,\delta,\rho)$. This is done by induction on the derivation tree in the intuitive way:
  \[
  \begin{array}{l}
  \mathsf{subst}_{\eta,\delta,\rho} : \set{[\Theta,n:N \mid \Delta,d:D \mid \Gamma,p:P]\ \varphi \prop} \\ \phantom{\mathsf{subst}_{\eta,\delta,\rho}}\hspace{-4pt}\nowidth[l]{$\to$}\hspace{12pt}\set{[\Theta \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma]\ \varphi \prop} \\
  \mathsf{subst}_{\eta,\delta,\rho}(\top) := \top, \mathsf{subst}(\bot) := \bot \\
  \mathsf{subst}_{\eta,\delta,\rho}(\psi \land \varphi) := \subst_{\eta,\delta,\rho}(\psi) \land \subst_{\eta,\delta,\rho}(\varphi) \\
  \mathsf{subst}_{\eta,\delta,\rho}(\psi \Rightarrow \varphi) := \subst_{\rho,\delta,\eta} (\psi) \Rightarrow \subst_{\eta,\delta,\rho} (\varphi) \\
  \mathsf{subst}_{\eta,\delta,\rho}(\exists^p x. \varphi(x,n,d,p)) := \exists^p x. \subst_{\eta,\delta,\rho}(\varphi(x,n,d,p)) \\
  \mathsf{subst}_{\eta,\delta,\rho}(\forall^p x. \varphi(x,n,d,p)) := \forall^p x. \subst_{\eta,\delta,\rho}(\varphi(x,n,d,p)) \\
  \mathsf{subst}_{\eta,\delta,\rho}(s(n,d) \le t(d,p)) := s(\eta,\delta) \le t(\delta,\rho) \\
  \mathsf{subst}_{\eta,\delta,\rho}(P(s(n,d) \mid t(d,p))) := P(s(\eta,\delta) \mid t(\delta,\rho)) \\
  \end{array}
  \]
  Note the inversion of the terms in the case of implication. In the case of polarized quantifiers we simply substitute under binders in the usual capture-avoidant way. We indicate with $s(\eta,\delta)$ the substitution of the term $\eta$ in the free variable $n:N$ (resp. $\delta$ in $d:D$) in the term $s(n,d)$, similarly defined inductively in the intuitive way; we omit this since terms and types are defined exactly as in simply-typed $\lambda$-calculus.
  This is similarly done for propositional contexts by applying substitution to each formula judgement appearing in the list.
\end{definition}
\begin{definition}[Theory]\label{def:theory}
A \emph{theory} $\Sigma$ is defined as a tuple $\Sigma = (\Sigma_B,\Sigma_F,\Sigma_E,\Sigma_P,\mathsf{EntClo}(\Sigma_A))$ which collects together data from all previous signatures, using $\mathsf{EntClo}$ to close the set of axioms $\Sigma_A$ under syntactic entailment \cite[3.2.5]{Jacobs1999categorical}.
\end{definition}

We now introduce the concepts of \emph{position, variance, and polarity}, and then describe the rules just presented while giving the fundamental intuition behind them.

\begin{definition}[Positions in a formula]
We use the name \emph{position} to indicate any point in which a (term) variable can appear in a formula, e.g., there are four possible positions $x,y,z,w$ for variables to appear in the FOL formula ``$x = y \land P(z,f(w))$''.
\end{definition}
\begin{definition}[Variance of a position]
Positions have a \emph{variance}, which can either be \emph{positive} or \emph{negative}: intuitively, a position starts out as positive, and flips between being positive and negative precisely in the following cases:
\begin{enumerate}
\item when it occurs on the left of the formula $x \le y$: e.g., the variable $x$ indicates a negative position in $``x \le c"$ and $``f(x) \le y"$;
\item when it occurs on the left of an implication formula $\psi \Rightarrow \varphi$, e.g., the position indicated by $x$ is negative in $``P(x) \Rightarrow \varphi(y)"$ and $``Q(f(x)) \Rightarrow \top"$;
\item when it occurs on the negative side $n$ of a predicate symbol $``P(n \mid p)"$, e.g., $x$ is negative in $``P(x,y \mid p,q)"$ and $``P(f(g(x),y) \mid f(z))"$.
\end{enumerate}
Variance can be inverted twice: for example, $x$ occurs \emph{positively} in the formulas $``x\le y \Rightarrow\!\varphi"$ and $``(y \le x \Rightarrow\!\varphi)\!\Rightarrow\!\varphi"$.
\end{definition}

From the semantic perspective of preorders, this flipping of variance corresponds with the presence of the opposite preorder $\Aop$ on the left side of functors ${\le_P} : P^\op \x P\>\I$, and $\Implication := {\le_\I} : \I^\op \times \I \> \I$ in particular. The fact that $(P^\op)^\op \equiv P$ justifies the fact that inverting a negative variable makes it positive again.
\begin{definition}[Polarity of a variable]
Variables can occur in multiple positions at the same time: we say that the \emph{polarity} of variable $x$ is either \emph{positive}, \emph{negative}, or \emph{dinatural}, in the following cases:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1em]
  \item \emph{positive} iff every position in which $x$ occurs is positive;
  \item \emph{negative} iff every position in which $x$ occurs is negative;
  \item \emph{dinatural} {iff\hspace{-0.3pt} every\hspace{-0.3pt} position\hspace{-0.3pt} in\hspace{-0.3pt} which\hspace{-0.3pt} $x$\hspace{-0.3pt} occurs\hspace{-0.3pt} is\hspace{-0.3pt} positive\hspace{-0.3pt} or\hspace{-0.3pt} negative (i.e., always: any variable is trivially dinatural).}
\end{itemize}
We will see in \Cref{thm:reindex_lift} how indeed \emph{any} variable can be ``lifted" to be considered dinatural. In practice, we shall use the more intuitive idea that a variable $x$ is called ``dinatural" iff it is neither positive nor negative, i.e., it occurs with both variances at the same time (or not at all).  A variable $x$ that is either positive or negative is said to be \emph{natural}. We denote the set of polarities  as $\set{-,\Delta,+}$.
\end{definition}
In logic and subtyping ''``co(ntra)variance" often refers to the polarity of \emph{propositional variables}, intuitively because entailments are contravariant on the left \cite{Castagna1995covariance}, similarly arising for negation $\neg A \Leftrightarrow (A \Rightarrow \bot)$. In directed first-order logic, this polarity aspect is also present at the level of \emph{variables}: in the preorder model, polarity corresponds intuitively to the fact that formulas admit a monotonicity property, e.g., if $x$ is a positive position in a formula $\varphi(x)$, then for any concrete $a,b \in P$ we have that $a \le b$ and $\varphi(a)$ imply $\varphi(b)$, i.e., $\varphi$ induces a covariant functor; viceversa, if $x$ is a negative position, then $a \le b$ and $\varphi(b)$ imply $\varphi(a)$, and $\varphi$ induces a contravariant functor \cite{MacLane1971categories}.

\begin{notation}[On overlines for variables]\label{rem:overlines_for_variables}
We will indicate with $\overline a:A$ in formulas $\varphi(\overline a,a,\overline b,b,...)$ to highlight points in which dinatural variables are used with different variances. These are just conveniences and do not exist syntactically. Positive and negative variables will either never or always have an overline, since they always unambiguously appear with the same variance.
\end{notation}
The main formal tool that we use to capture precisely this idea of variance involves dividing contexts in terms of the possible polarity of variables, which we call \emph{polarized contexts}. This technique lends itself particularly well to captured by the \emph{doctrinal} semantics, which similarly puts particular emphasis on the role of contexts and free variables for formulas \cite{Jacobs1999categorical}.
\begin{definition}[Polarized context]\label{def:polarized_context}
  A \emph{polarized context} is a triple of contexts $[\Theta \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma]$ for which $\Theta,\Delta,\Gamma\ctx$, where, intuitively, $\Theta$ is a list of variables that can be used only negatively, variables in $\Delta$ are dinatural (i.e., can be used either positively or negatively in any position), and variables in $\Gamma$ only positively. We will occasionaly abbreviate definitions by parameterizing them w.r.t. a polarity $p \in \set{-,\Delta,+}$: for example, \makebox{$[\Theta \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma],[x :^p A]$} denotes the extended polarized context obtained by appending a variable $x$ of type $A$ to the correct context.
  We denote with $\ectx$ the empty context.
\end{definition}

\noindent We now describe the main rules of the logic, both in formula construction and entailments.

\paragraph{(Predicate symbols for formulas.)}
Polarized contexts are particularly relevant in the base formulas $s \le_A t$ and $P(n \mid p)$, where $s,t$ are terms. In particular, $s$ is typed in context $\Theta,\Delta$, and $t$ in context $\Gamma,\Delta$: the core idea behind this definition is that positive positions in formulas can be filled \emph{either} by a \emph{positive} variable, or by a \emph{dinatural} one, i.e., in the case in which a term $s$ is a variable ${\Theta,\Delta \vdash x : A}$ for some $A$, either ${(x:A) \in \Theta}$ or ${(x:A) \in \Delta}$. This rule allows for the specific kind of reindexing of variables used in the rule $\Rulereindex$, as well as in the categorical semantics in \Cref{sec:doctrinal_semantics}.
\paragraph{(Polarized exponentials.)}\label{def:polarized_exponentials}
In directed first-order logic, implication of formulas must be given a special treatment due to the contravariance of $\Rightarrow$ in its first argument. This behaviour is similarly mirrored in the case of preorders, due to the presence of $\I^\op$ in the semantics for implication $\Implication : \I^\op \x \I \> \I$.
This is implemented in two judgements: when constructing formulas $[\Theta\mid\Delta\mid\Gamma]\ \psi \Rightarrow \varphi \prop$, and when currying entailments in the rule $\Ruleimpl$. We use the name \emph{polarized exponentials} to reflect the inversion of polarity for variables: given $\psi \Rightarrow \varphi$, we swap the negative and positive context of the formula $\psi$ on the left side. A similar idea is applied for currying in entailments, since, as briefly illustrated in \Cref{sec:intro:equality_syntactically}, currying a formula $\psi$ \emph{inverts} the polarity of all the variables in $\psi$. For instance, consider a formula $\psi(x,y,z)$, with $x,y$ negative positions and $z$ positive, and take the entailment $\psi(x,\n y,z) \land \Phi(y) \vdash \varphi(z)$ where $x$ is negative, $y$ dinatural (negative in $\psi$ and positive in $\Phi$), $z$ positive; by currying, this is equivalent to $\Phi(y) \vdash \psi(x,y,\n z) \Rightarrow \varphi(z)$, where now $x$ appears \emph{positively} on the right side of the entailment, $z$ now becomes dinatural (due to the positive appearance in $\varphi$ and the negative appearance in $\psi$, which is now on the left side of $\Rightarrow$), and $y$ becomes positive.

This behaviour of managing polarity of variables is implemented by the rule $\Ruleimpl$: the intuition is that we consider both the case in which variables which are natural on the top side (the contexts $N,P$) become dinatural on the bottom side, \emph{and viceversa} (the contexts $N',P'$). The fact that positive variables can directly switch to negative is captured in the derived rule $\Ruleimplvarchange$. Note the absence of a general context $\Theta,\Gamma$ in $\psi$: this is because \emph{all} the variables of $\psi$ change polarity, and the only ones that do not are those in $\Delta$ since they already appear with both variances.

\paragraph{(Directed equality.)}\label{def:directed_equality}
The formula $a \le_A b$ (where $a,b$ are positions with negative and positive variance, respectively) is the main construct of directed first-order logic, and it is a proposition stating that $a$ ``rewrites" in $b$: in the preorder model, this is indeed represented by the fact that the relation $a \le b$ holds. We illustrate the intuition behind the rule for directed equality $\Rulele$: in the $\Downarrow$ direction, the rule states that a directed equality $a \le b$ in context can be contracted \emph{only if} $a$ and $b$ only appear \emph{naturally} in the conclusion; thanks to the presence of polarized exponentials, (as well as in the preorder model) $a$ and $b$ must appear with the opposite variance in the hypothesis context $\Rulelefull$. We show in \Cref{rem:failure_symmetry} that this syntactic constraint does not allow for symmetry to be derived.

\paragraph{(Polarized quantifiers.)}\label{def:polarized_quantifiers}
Since polarized contexts keep track of the polarity of variables, quantifiers also need to track this information: we define polarized quantifiers $\forall^+.\phi(x)$, $\forall^-x.\phi(x)$, $\forall^\Delta x.\phi(\nxx)$, similarly for exists $\exists^p.\phi(x)$ for $p \in \set{-,\Delta,+}$. The rules for polarized quantifiers are captured in $\Ruleforall$ and $\Ruleexists$.


\begin{remark}[On admissibility of substitution in entailments]
\label{rem:adm_subst_entailments}
Substitution in formulas of \Cref{def:substitution_in_formulas} is used in $\Rulereindex$. In other syntactic accounts of logic (e.g., \cite{Shulman2016categorical}) this rule is often admissible rather than assumed, but it requires rules for entailments to be parametric on terms. We use this equivalent presentation of rules in adjoint form to make the doctrinal semantics more intuitive.
\end{remark}

\section{Examples}\label{sec:examples}

In this section we provide derived rules and then use them in examples that exemplify the properties of directed equality and polarized exponentials/quantifiers.

\begin{example}[Derived rules]\label{ex:derived_rules}
The following rules are derivable; we report here only some statements, leaving the complete derivations and other examples in $\Cref{appendix:derived_rules}$. The following rule and $\Rulelerefl$ state reflexivity of directed equality, and they are equivalent to the $\Uparrow$ direction of $\Rulele$.
\[
\begin{prooftree}
  \hypo{\Delta \vdash t : A}
  \infer1[\Rulereflterm]{[\Theta \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma]\ \Phi \vdash t \le_A t}
\end{prooftree}
\]
\LinkRulereflterm%
Note that $t$ in $\Rulereflterm$ must not depend on any natural variable, since they would have to appear with both variances on the two sides of $\le$; this is mirrored by reindexing. We give a rule for directed equality contraction which takes into account the polarity of the variables contracted (in $\Rulele$ they always become dinatural). $\Ruleleminus$ is given similarly.
\[
\begin{prooftree}
  \infer[no rule]0{\hspace{-9.72em}[\Theta\mid\Delta \mid \Gamma,a:A]\ \Phi \propctx, \varphi \prop}
  \infer[no rule]1{[\Theta \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma, z:A]\ \takespace{\n a \le b,\Phi(a)}{\Phi(z)} & \vdash \varphi(z)}
  \infer[double]1[\Ruleleplus]{[\Theta \mid \Delta,a:A \mid \Gamma,b:A]\ {\n a \le b,\Phi(a)} & \vdash \varphi(b)}
\end{prooftree}
\]
\LinkRuleleplus
Using polarized exponentials the following general principle for directed equality elimination can be derived, with an additional propositional context $\Phi$ in which, however, $a,b$ must appear \emph{negatively}. This is reminiscent of the Frobenius equality formulation for equality \cite[3.2.4]{Jacobs1999categorical}.
\[
\begin{prooftree}
  \hypo{[\Theta \mid \Delta, z:A \mid \Gamma]\ \takespace{a \le b,\Phi(\nanb)}{\Phi(\znz)} & \vdash \varphi(\nzz)}
  \infer[double]1[\Rulelefull]{[\Theta \mid \Delta,a:A,b:A \mid \Gamma]\ {a \le b,\Phi(\nanb)} & \vdash \varphi(a,b)}
\end{prooftree}
\]
\LinkRulelefull
The following more general directed equality elimination with terms can be derived using $\Rulereindex$:
\[
\begin{prooftree}
  \infer[no rule]0{\hspace{-9.48em}\Theta,\Delta \vdash \eta : A, \quad \Gamma,\Delta \vdash \rho : A}
  \infer[no rule]1{\hspace{-9.48em}[\Theta,a:A\mid \Delta \mid \Gamma,b:A]\ \Phi(a,b) \propctx,\varphi(a,b) \prop}
  \infer[no rule]1{[\Theta, z:A \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma]\ \takespace{{\eta \le_A \rho},\,\Phi(\eta,\rho)}{\Phi(\n z,z)} & \vdash \varphi(\n z,z)}
  \infer[double]1[\Ruleleterms]{\takespace[r]{[\Theta, z:A \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma]}{[\Theta \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma]}\ {\eta \le_A \rho},\,\Phi(\eta,\rho)& \vdash \varphi(\eta,\rho)}
\end{prooftree}
\]
\LinkRuleleterms
Note that the syntactic restriction here is still captured by requiring for \emph{natural} occurrences of $a:A,b:A$ in $\Phi,\varphi$.

The typical rules for quantifiers of FOL can be derived from the adjoint formulation $\Ruleexists,\Ruleforall$ using $\Rulecut$ and $\Rulereindex$; we report here only the rules used in examples, leaving the full set of rules and their proofs in \Cref{appendix:derived_rules}:
\[
\begin{prooftree}
\hypo{\Theta,\Delta \vdash \eta : N}
\hypo{[\Theta \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma]\ \Phi \vdash \psi(\eta)}
\infer2[\Ruleexiststermminus]{[\Theta \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma]\ \Phi \vdash \exists^- x.\psi(x)}
\end{prooftree}
\]
\[
\begin{prooftree}
\hypo{\Delta \vdash \delta : D}
\hypo{[\Theta \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma]\ \Phi \vdash \forall^\Delta x.\psi(x)}
\infer2[\Ruleforalltermdelta]{[\Theta \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma]\ \Phi \vdash \psi(\delta,\delta)}
\end{prooftree}
\]%
\LinkRuleexiststermminus%
\LinkRuleforalltermdelta%

\end{example}
A rule $\Ruleimplvarchange$ which directly swaps a variable from negative to positive can be derived $\Ruleimpl$.
\begin{example}[Derivations]\label{ex:derivations}
We illustrate some derivations with directed first-order logic, taking as reference the examples in $\Cref{sec:intro:equality_syntactically}$. We consider a simple signature with two base types $A,B \in \Sigma_A$, a function symbol $f \in \Sigma_F$ for which $\mathsf{dom}(f) := A, \mathsf{cod}(f) := B$, and a predicate symbol $P \in \Sigma_P$ for which $\mathsf{pos}(P) := A, \mathsf{neg}(P) := \top$.
\begin{itemize}
\item Congruence of directed equality (i.e. internal monotonicity for terms):
\[
\begin{prooftree}
\infer0[\Rulereflterm]{[\,\ectx \mid z:A \mid \ectx\,]\ \takespace{a \le_A b}{} & \vdash f(\n z) \le_B f(z)}
\infer1[\Rulele]{[a:A \mid \ectx \mid b:A]\ a \le_A b & \vdash f (a) \le_B f (b)}
\end{prooftree}
\]
\item Transport of equalities between proofs of predicates: (i.e. internal monotonicity for predicates):
\[
\begin{prooftree}
\infer0[\Rulehyp]{[\,\ectx\mid \ectx \mid z:A \,]\ \takespace{a \le b,\,P(a)}{P(z)} & \vdash P(z)}
\infer1[\Ruleleplus]{ [a:A\mid \,\ectx\mid b:A]\ a \le b,\,P(a) & \vdash P(b)}
\end{prooftree}
\]
\item Transitivity of directed equality:
\[
\begin{prooftree}
\infer0[\Rulehyp]{[ \,z:A \mid \ectx \mid c:A]\ \takespace{a \le b,\,\n b \le c}{z \le c} & \vdash z \le c}
\infer1[\Ruleleminus]{[a:A \mid b:A \mid c:A]\ a \le b,\,\n b \le c & \vdash a \le c}
\end{prooftree}
\]
\item Existence of singletons:
\[
\begin{prooftree}
\infer0[\Rulelerefl]{[\,\ectx \mid y:A \mid \takespace[c]{y:A}{\ectx}] \  & \vdash \n y \le y}
\infer1[\Ruleexiststermminus]{[\,\ectx \mid \takespace[c]{y:A}{\ectx} \mid y:A] \  & \vdash \exists^- x. x \le y}
\infer1[\Ruleforall]{[\,\ectx \mid \takespace[c]{y:A}{\ectx} \mid \takespace[c]{y:A}{\ectx}] \  & \vdash \forall^+ y.\exists^- x. x \le y}
\end{prooftree}
\]
\item Pair of rewrites:
\[
\begin{prooftree}
\infer0[\Rulereflterm]{[\,\ectx\mid x:A,y:B \mid \ectx\,]\ \vdash (\n x,\n y) \le_{A \times B} (x,y)}
\infer1[\Rulele]{[b:A \mid x:A \mid b':B]\ b \le_B b' \vdash (\n x,b) \le_{A \times B} (x,b')}
\infer1[\Rulele]{[a:A,b:A \mid \,\ectx\, \mid a':A,b':B]\ a \le_A a',\ b \le_B b'}
\infer[no rule]1{\phantom{[a:A,b:A \mid \,\ectx\, \mid a':A,b':B]\ \hspace{-11pt}}\vdash (a,b) \le_{A \times B} (a',b')}
\end{prooftree}
\]
The other direction (``directed injectivity of pairs") follows from \emph{congruence of directed equality} using projections $\pi_1,\pi_2$ and the term equalities.
\item Higher-order rewriting:
\[
\begin{prooftree}
\infer0[\Rulereflterm]{[\,\ectx \mid h:A\Rightarrow B,x:A \mid \ectx\,]\ \vdash \n h \cdot \n x \le_B h \cdot x}
\infer1[\Ruleforalltermdelta]{[\,\ectx \mid h:A\Rightarrow B \mid \ectx\,]\ \vdash \forall^\Delta x.\ \n h \cdot \n x \le_B h \cdot x}
\infer1[\Rulele]{[f:A\Rightarrow B \mid \ectx \mid g:A \Rightarrow B] \ \takespace[l]{\forall^\Delta x. f \cdot x \le_B g \cdot x}{f \le_{A \Rightarrow B} g}}
\infer[no rule]1{\phantom{[f:A\Rightarrow B \mid \ectx \mid g:A \Rightarrow B]\ \hspace{-11pt}}\vdash \forall^\Delta x.\ f \cdot \n x \le_B g \cdot x}
\end{prooftree}
\]
The other direction is not derivable in general, since it captures a suitable notion of ``2-dimensional extensionality" \cite{Hofmann1997extensional} and, in particular, it also would capture an $\Leftrightarrow$-extensionality.
\end{itemize}
\end{example}
\begin{remark}[Failure of symmetry]\label{rem:failure_symmetry}
We illustrate how symmetry of directed equality is not derivable in this logic, and indeed the $\Preord$ model is a countermodel: the entailment
\[
\begin{prooftree}
\infer0{[ \,\ectx \mid a:A,b:A \mid \ectx\,]\ a \le b \vdash \n b \le \n a}
\end{prooftree}
\]
can only be given type in the term context where both $a$ and $b$ appear dinaturally, since $[a:A\mid \ectx \mid b:A]\ a \le b\ \prop$ and $[b:A\mid \ectx \mid a:A]\ b \le a\ \prop$ must be weakened in the same context, namely $[\,\ectx \mid a,b:A \mid  \ectx\,]$, for the judgement to be well-formed.
\end{remark}

The following special cases of $\mathsf{subst}_{\eta,\delta,\rho}$ will be instructive for the categorical semantics in \Cref{sec:doctrinal_semantics}.
\begin{theorem}[Lifting natural to dinatural]\label{thm:reindex_lift}
Any variable appearing naturally can be lifted to be dinatural: there is a function on sets of formulas defined by $\mathsf{lift}_{\Theta,\Delta,\Gamma,N,P} := \subst_{\eta_-,\pi_1,\rho_+} : \set{[\Theta,n:N \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma,p:P]\ \varphi \prop} \to \set{[\Theta \mid \Delta,n:N,p:P \mid \Gamma]\ \varphi \prop}$, where $\eta_- := (\Theta,\Delta,n:N,p:P \vdash n : N)$, $\Delta,n:N,p:P \vdash \pi_1 : \Delta$, $\rho_+ := (\Gamma,\Delta,n:N,p:P \vdash p : P)$. We use $\Delta \iso [\Delta,a:\top]$ to add a dummy variable for $\mathsf{subst}$ whenever we do not need to substitute a variable in context.
\end{theorem}
\begin{theorem}[Collapsing two naturals in one dinatural]
  \label{thm:reindex_collapse}
  A formula with two natural variables $\varphi(a,b)$ can be collapsed to one $\varphi(\n x,x)$ in the context with a single dinatural $x$: there is a function on formulas $\mathsf{contract}_{\Theta\Delta\Gamma A} := \subst_{\eta_\Delta,\pi_1,\rho_\Delta}$ $: {\set{[\Theta,n\!:\!A \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma,p\!:\!A]\ \varphi \prop}\!\to\!\set{[\Theta \mid \Delta,a\!:\!A \mid \Gamma]\ \varphi \prop}}$, where $\eta_\Delta := (\Theta,\Delta,a:A \vdash a : A)$, $\pi_1 := (\Delta,a:A \vdash a : \Delta)$, and $\rho_\Delta := (\Gamma,\Delta,a:A \vdash a : A)$. Moreover, this contraction on formulas is functorial on entailments:\[
  \begin{prooftree}
    \infer[no rule]0{[\Theta,n:A \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma,p:A]\ \Phi(n,p) \vdash \varphi(n,p)}
    \infer1[\Rulereindex]{[\Theta \mid \Delta,a:A \mid \Gamma]\ \Phi(\nzz) \vdash \varphi(\nzz)}
    \end{prooftree}
  \]
  This contraction operation is crucial for the characterization of directed equality as adjoint in \Cref{def:sem:directed_doctrine}.
  \end{theorem}
  We remark how directed equivalence of elements still does \emph{not} in general recover symmetric equality:
  \begin{remark}[Symmetric equality]\label{thm:bidirectional_symmetric_equality}
  In the entailment \[
    \begin{prooftree}
      \infer[no rule]0{[\Theta \mid \Delta,a:A,b:A \mid \Gamma]\ {a \le_A b \land \n b \le_A \n a,\Phi} & \vdash \varphi(\n a,a,\n b,b)}
    \end{prooftree}
    \]\LinkRuleiff%
  one cannot contract the directed equalities in context unless $a,b$ also appear naturally in $\varphi$; in such a case, one of the two equalities can be moved on the right side using polarized exponentials, and then the other one can be contracted.
  \end{remark}

  We give some examples of signatures to exemplify how directed first-order logic can be used to model directed structure. A suitable extension of directed first-order logic (e.g., a two-level logical system in the style of \cite{New2023formal}) could be used to show non-trivial theorems.

\begin{example}[Theory of $\lambda$-terms]
We capture a signature of untyped $\lambda$-terms in the style of HOAS \cite{Fiore1999abstract}, as follows:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\Sigma_A := \set{T}$ with a type of $\lambda$-terms,
  \item $\Sigma_F\!:=\!\set{\llambda,\app}$ for $\lambda$-abstraction and application with:
\end{itemize}
\vspace{2pt}
\[\begin{array}{l@{\ }l@{\ }l@{\ }l}
  \dom(\llambda)&:= T \Rightarrow T,                  & \cod(\llambda)&:=T\\
  \dom(\app)   &:= T \times T, & \cod(\app)&:=T \\
  \end{array}\]
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\Sigma_E := \set{\eta}$, such that:
\end{itemize}
  \[
  \begin{prooftree}
    \infer0[$(\eta)$]{[f:T \Rightarrow T]\ \left(\lambda x.\app(\llambda(f),x)\right) = f : T \Rightarrow T}
  \end{prooftree}
  \]
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\Sigma_P := \set{},\Sigma_A := \set{\beta}$, such that:
\end{itemize}
\[
  \begin{prooftree}
   \infer0[$(\beta)$]{[\,\ectx\mid s:T\Rightarrow T, t:T \mid\ectx\,]\ \app(\llambda(\n s),\n t) \le s \cdot t}
  \end{prooftree}
\]
Note that from this simple signature we automatically have that $\app$ and $\lambda$ are congruences w.r.t. the reduction relation represented by directed equality:
\[
\begin{prooftree}
  \infer0[\Rulereflterm]{[\,\ectx\mid z:T,t:T \mid\ectx\,]\ \vdash \app(\n z,\n t) \le_{T} \app(z,t)}
  \infer1[\Rulele]{[s:T \mid t:T \mid s':T]\ s \le_T s' \vdash \app(s,\n t) \le_{T} \app(s',t)}
\end{prooftree}
\]
Semantically, the type $T$ can be interpreted by the preorder of $\lambda$-terms ordered by $\beta$-relation, as an example of the semantics given in \Cref{sec:doctrinal_semantics}.
\end{example}
\begin{example}[Theory of $\omega\text{-CPOs}_\bot$]
We capture a signature of $\omega\text{-CPOs}_\bot$ from domain theory \cite{Fiore1997category}, as follows:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\Sigma_A := \set{D,\mathbb{N}}$, representing, in the preorder model, a domain $D$ and the discrete preorder of naturals $\mathbb{N}$.
  \item $\Sigma_F := \set{0,\mathsf{succ}}$ defined in the intuitive way, $\Sigma_E := \set{}, \Sigma_P := \set{}$, $\Sigma_A := \set{\bot_{\mathsf{axiom}},\bigsqcup_{\mathsf{axiom}}}$, for which, (omitting the empty term context):
\end{itemize}
  \[
  \begin{prooftree}
   \infer0[$(\bot_{\mathsf{axiom}})$]{\ \vdash \exists^- b.\ \forall^+ x.\ b \le_D x}
  \end{prooftree}
  \]
  \[
  \begin{prooftree}
   \infer0[$(\bigsqcup_{\mathsf{axiom}})$]{
    \begin{array}{l}
      \vdash \forall^\Delta(c:\mathbb{N} \Rightarrow D).(\forall^\Delta(i:\mathbb{N}).\ \n c \cdot \n i \le c \cdot \mathsf{succ}(i)) \\
       \hspace{18pt}\Rightarrow (\exists^\Delta (b:D).\ (\forall^-(i:\mathbb{N}).\ \n c \cdot i \le b) \\
       \hspace{22pt}\land\ (\forall^+(b':D). (\forall^-(i:\mathbb{N}).\ \n c \cdot i \le b\nowidth{$) \Rightarrow b \le b'))$}
    \end{array}}
  \end{prooftree}
  \]
\end{example}

\section{Doctrinal Semantics}\label{sec:doctrinal_semantics}
In this section we overview the doctrinal semantics of directed first-order logic.
The crucial aspect is the fact that reindexing in directed first-order logic is given precisely as in \Cref{rule:reindex}, which captures exactly the way in which variables of a formula $\varphi$ can be supplied. The way to capture this is by requiring for the doctrine to have a specific reindexing structure, which we simply implement by changing the base category. Later, we ask for certain adjunctions or rules to hold with respect to these specific reindexings. The intuition for the polarization category is that it captures precisely the reindexing action in \Cref{def:substitution_in_formulas}, interpreting concatenation of contexts via products.

\begin{definition}
  Given a category $\C$ with finite products, we define the \emph{polarization category of $\C$}, denoted as $\ndp{\C}$:
  \begin{itemize}
  \item Objects: triples of objects \[(\Theta \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma) \in \C_0\times\C_0\times\C_0\]
  \item Morphisms $(\Theta\mid\Delta\mid\Gamma) \> (\Theta'\mid\Delta'\mid\Gamma')$ are triples
    \[(n : \Theta \times \Delta \> \Theta' \mid d : \Delta \> \Delta' \mid p : \Gamma \times \Delta \> \Gamma')\]
  \item Identities are given by $(\pi_1 \mid \id \mid \pi_1)$.
  \item Composition: $(n \mid d \mid p) \< (n'\mid  d'\mid  p')$ is defined as
  \[
    (\ang{n,\pi_2 \< d} \< n' \hspace{6pt} \mid\hspace{6pt}  d \< d'\hspace{6pt}  \mid\hspace{6pt} \ang{p,\pi_2 \< d} \< p').\]
 \end{itemize}
  where we will always use composition $f \< g$ in diagrammatic order, and $\ang{f,g}$ is the universal map of cartesian products.
\end{definition}
\begin{definition}[$\ndp{\C}$ as functor]
The above definition lifts to a functor $\ndp{-} : \CC \> \CC$ on the category of small categories with finite products $\CC$, i.e., any product-preserving functor $F : \C \> \D$ induces a functor $\ndp{F} : \ndp{\C} \> \ndp{\D}$ defined in the intuitive way $(\Theta\mid \Delta\mid \Gamma) \mapsto (F(\Theta)\mid F(\Delta)\mid F(\Gamma))$ and similarly on morphisms.
\end{definition}
\noindent A technical condition for doctrines is necessary because of the base case for predicates ${P(s \mid t)}$; this is the only non-standard aspect in the construction of syntactic doctrines \cite{Jacobs1999categorical}, hence the condition involving objects in fibers.
\begin{definition}[No-dinatural-variance condition]\label{def:no_dinat_variance}
A functor $\cP : \ndp{\C}^\op \> \Pos$ is said to satisfy the \emph{(strict) no-dinatural-variance condition} if the ``full dinatural collapse" functor $\cP(\fulllift{\Delta}) : \cP(\Theta \times \Delta \mid \top \mid \Gamma \times \Delta) \> \cP(\Theta\mid \Delta\mid\Gamma)$ induced by reindexing with $\fulllift{\Delta} := (\id_{\Theta \times \Delta} \mid {!_\Delta} \mid \id_{\Gamma \times \Delta})$ is a \emph{bijection on objects}, denoting with ${\eps : \cP(\Theta\mid \Delta\mid\Gamma)_0 \to \cP(\Theta \times \Delta \mid \top \mid \Gamma \times \Delta)_0}$ the inverse function of sets with $\cP(\fulllift{\Delta})(\eps(p)) = p$.
\end{definition}
\noindent This is usually \emph{not} an isomorphism of posets since, in the syntactic model, \[{\forall^\Delta d.\varphi(\n a,d,\n d,\n b)\Rightarrow\varphi(a,\n d,d,b)}\] might not imply that \[{\forall^+ d. \forall^- d'.\varphi(\n a,\n d',\n d,\n b)\Rightarrow\varphi(a,d',d,b)}.\] The converse implication holds by $\cP(\fulllift{\Delta})$.
The intuition is that, at the level of formulas, dinatural variables only play a structural role, and there is ``no third kind" of variance since predicates only depend on two kinds of variables, and any dinatural $x:A$ in a formula arises as a dinatural collapse. This requirement arises only at the level of predicates, since in the base case $P(s \mid t)$ we do not ask for a type ``$\textsf{dinat}(\sigma)$" (and term $\Delta \vdash \textsf{dinat}(\sigma)$), but only $\textsf{pos}(\sigma),\textsf{neg}(\sigma)$ and $s,t$.

\begin{definition}[Polarized doctrine]\label{thm:polarized_doctrine}
A (split) \emph{polarized doctrine} is defined as a cartesian closed category $\C$ equipped with a functor ${\cP : \ndp{\C}^\op \> \Pos}$ satisfying the \emph{no-dinatural-variance} condition.
\end{definition}
\begin{definition}[Weakenings]\label{thm:sem:weakening}
Given a polarity $p \in \set{-,\Delta,+}$ we shall denote with $\cP(\Theta \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma \mid\mid^p A)$ the fiber obtained by applying the functor $- \times A$ to either $\Theta,\Delta,\Gamma$ depending on $p$ in the intuitive way. We denote weakening functors by $\wk^p_A : \cP(\Theta \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma) \> \cP(\Theta \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma \mid\mid^p A)$.
\end{definition}

We need semantic equivalents for \Cref{thm:reindex_lift,thm:reindex_collapse} in order to later characterize logical connectives:
\begin{theorem}[Dinatural lift]\label{thm:sem:reindex_lift}
  There is a functor \[\cP({\lift{N,P}}) : \cP(\Theta \times N \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma \times P) \to \cP(\Theta\mid\Delta\times N \times P\mid\Gamma)\] given by reindexing with the term \[
  \begin{array}{r@{\ }l}
    {\lift{N,P}} := ( & \ang{\pi_1,\pi_3} : \Theta \times \Delta \times N \times P \> \Theta \times N, \\
    \mid & \pi_1 : {\Delta \times N \times P \> \Delta}, \\
    \mid & \ang{\pi_1,\pi_4} : {\Gamma \times \Delta \times N \times P} \> \Gamma \times P) \\
    : & (\Theta\mid\Delta\times N \times P\mid\Gamma) \> (\Theta \times N \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma \times P).
  \end{array}\]
  One-variable versions $\lift{N},\lift{P}$ can be given similarly. Moreover, $\lift{N,P}\<(\pi_1 \mid {!_\Delta} \mid \pi_1) = \fulllift{\Delta}$.
  \end{theorem}
  \begin{theorem}[Dinatural contract]\label{thm:sem:reindex_collapse}
    There is a functor \[\cP({\contract{A}}) : \cP(\Theta \times A\mid\Delta\mid\Gamma \times A) \to \cP(\Theta\mid\Delta \times A\mid\Gamma)\] given by reindexing with the term
    \[
    \begin{array}{r@{\ }l}
      \contract{A} := ( & \ang{\pi_1,\pi_3} : \Theta \times \Delta \times A \to \Theta \times A, \\
      \mid & \pi_1 : \Delta \times A \to \Delta, \\
      \mid & \ang{\pi_1,\pi_3} : \Gamma \times \Delta \times A \to \Gamma \times A) \\
      : & (\Theta\mid \Delta \times A \mid\Gamma) \>  (\Theta \times A\mid \Delta \mid\Gamma \times A).
    \end{array}
    \]
    \end{theorem}

\begin{definition}[Logical connectives for polarized doctrines]
  \label{def:sem:directed_doctrine}
  We define conditions on a polarized doctrine $\cP : \ndp{\C}^\op \> \Pos$. For each definition we also ask that each reindexing functor preserves the relevant structure.
  \begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.2em]
  \item \emph{$\cP$ has conjunctions} iff each fiber has finite products (i.e., greatest lower bounds as a preorder). By a standard argument \cite{Jacobs1999categorical} this induces a conjunction functor $\Conjunction : \cP(\Theta\mid\Delta\mid\Gamma) \times \cP(\Theta\mid\Delta\mid\Gamma) \to \cP(\Theta\mid\Delta\mid\Gamma)$.
  \item \emph{$\cP$ has polarized exponentials \Ruleimpl} iff there is a functor \[
  \begin{array}{r@{\,}l}
  \Implication : & \cP(N \times N' \mid \Delta \mid P \times P')^\op \\ \times & \cP(\Theta \times N \times P'\mid \Delta\mid\Gamma \times P \times N') \\ \to & \cP(\Theta \times P' \mid \Delta \times N \times P \mid \Gamma \times N') \\
  \end{array}
  \]
  such that, for every $\Theta,\Delta,\Gamma,N,N',P,P'\in \C$ and $\Phi, \varphi \in\cP(\Theta \times N \times P'\mid \Delta\mid\Gamma \times P \times N')$, $\psi \in \cP(N \times N' \mid \Delta \mid P \times P')$, the top relation holds iff the bottom one holds:
  \[
  \begin{prooftree}
    \infer[no rule]0{\cP(\pi_2,\id,\pi_2)(\cP(\lift{N',P'})(\psi)) \times \cP(\lift{N',P'})(\Phi) \le \cP({\lift{N',P'}})(\varphi)}
    \infer[double]1{\cP(\lift{N,P})(\Phi) \le \psi \Rightarrow \varphi}
  \end{prooftree}
  \]
  where the top entailment takes place in $\cP(\Theta \times N \mid \Delta \times N' \times P' \mid \Gamma \times P)$ and the bottom in $\cP(\Theta \times P' \mid \Delta \times N \times P \mid \Gamma \times N')$.
  We remark in \Cref{thm:uniqueness_pol_exp} that having polarized exponentials is property and not structure.
  \item \emph{$\cP$ has polarized quantifiers} iff for every $p\in\set{-,\Delta,+}$ and $A \in \C$ the functor $\wk^p_{A} : \cP(\Theta \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma) \> \cP(\Theta\mid \Delta\mid \Gamma \mid\mid^p A)$ has a left adjoint $\exists^p_{A}$ and a right adjoint $\forall^p_{A}$, i.e., the adjunction $\exists^p_{A} \dashv \wk^p_{A} \dashv \forall^p_{A}$ holds.
  Moreover, suitable Beck-Chevalley conditions are satisfied, i.e., the following points of $\cP(\Theta \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma)$ are equal for any $f : (\Theta\mid\Delta\mid\Gamma) \> (\Theta'\mid\Delta'\mid\Gamma')$ and $\varphi \in \cP(\Theta' \mid \Delta' \mid \Gamma' \mid\mid^p A)$:
  \[
  \begin{array}{c}
    \cP(f)(\exists^p_{A[\Theta'\Delta'\Gamma']}(\varphi))
    = \exists^p_{A[\Theta\Delta\Gamma]}(\cP(f \mid\mid^p \id_A)(\varphi)) \\
    \cP(f)(\forall^p_{A[\Theta'\Delta'\Gamma']}(\varphi))
    = \forall^p_{A[\Theta\Delta\Gamma]}(\cP(f \mid\mid^p \id_A)(\varphi)) \\
  \end{array}
  \]
  where the morphism $(f \mid\mid^p \id_A) : (\Theta\mid\Delta\mid\Gamma\mid\mid^p A) \> (\Theta'\mid\Delta'\mid\Gamma'\mid\mid^p A)$ reindexes the relevant variables with $F$ and leaves $A$ untouched.
  We omit an intuitive Frobenius condition which in this case holds automatically thanks to the presence of exponentials, following a similar proof in \cite[1.9.12(i)]{Jacobs1999categorical}.
  \item \emph{$\cP$ has directed equality} iff, given the following functors,
  \[
  \begin{array}{rl}
    \cP(\contract{A}) & : \cP(\Theta \times A \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma \times A) \> \cP(\Theta \mid \Delta \times A \mid \Gamma) \\
    \cP(\wk^\Delta_A) & : \cP(\Theta \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma) \> \cP(\Theta \mid \Delta \times A \mid \Gamma)
  \end{array}
  \]
  there is a $\cP(\wk^\Delta_A)$-relative left adjoint ${{\le_A}\times-}$ to the contraction functor $\cP(\contract{A})$, i.e.,
  \[
    \xymatrix{
    & \takespace[c]{\cP(\Theta \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma)}{\cP(\Theta \times A \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma \times A)}\ar[dr]^{\cP(\contract{A})}\ar@{}[d]|(.6){\dashv} & \\
    \cP(\Theta \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma) \ar[ur]^{{{\le_A}\times-}} \ar[rr]_{\cP(\wk^\Delta_A)} & & \cP(\Theta \mid \Delta \times A \mid \Gamma)
    }
  \]
  Details on relative adjunctions for categories are given in \Cref{appendix:left_relative_adjunction}, \cite{Arkor2024formal}. In our case of interest, posets, such condition reduces to the following bi-implication of conditions for $\Phi \in \cP(\Theta \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma),\varphi \in \cP(\Theta \times A \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma \times A)$:
  \[
  \begin{prooftree}
    \hypo{\ \takespace{a \le b,\Phi}{[\Theta \mid \Delta \times A \mid \Gamma]\ \cP(\wk^\Delta_A)(\Phi)} & \le \cP(\contract{A})(\varphi)}
    \infer[double]1[\Rulele]{[\Theta \times A \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma \times A]\ {{\le_A}\times \Phi} & \le \varphi}
  \end{prooftree}
  \]
  Moreover, we ask for the following Beck-Chevalley condition \cite[3.4.1]{Jacobs1999categorical}; for any map $f := (n \mid d \mid p) : (\Theta\mid\Delta\mid\Gamma) \> (\Theta'\mid\Delta'\mid\Gamma')$ and $\varphi \in \cP(\Theta' \mid \Delta' \mid \Gamma')$, the following points in $\cP(\Theta \times A \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma \times A)$ are asked to be equivalent:
   \[
      \le_{A[\Theta\Delta\Gamma]}\!(\cP(f)(\varphi))
      \Leftrightarrow \cP(n \times \id_A\!\mid d \mid p \times \id_A)(\le_{A[\Theta'\Delta'\Gamma]}\!(\varphi)),
    \]
  where $\cP(n\!\times\!\id_A\!\mid\!d\!\mid\!p\!\times\!\id_A) : \cP(\Theta'\times A \mid \Delta' \mid \Gamma' \times A) \to \cP(\Theta\times A \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma \times A)$ is defined in the intuitive way by applying $f$ and leaving the variables $A$ unaltered.
  A similar ``polarized Frobenius" condition, exemplified in $\Rulelefull$, holds using polarized exponentials \cite[1.9.12(i)]{Jacobs1999categorical}.
  \end{itemize}
  \end{definition}
  \noindent Note the similarity between the above left-relative adjunction and standard accounts of equality in categorical logic \cite{Jacobs1999categorical,Maietti2013quotient,Maietti2023characterization}. In our case we cannot relate directed equality to existentials and their characterization as left adjoints \cite{Maietti2015unifying}, since the former is given by a \emph{relative} adjunction and the latter by a standard adjunction, respectively.

  A binary attribute of mixed variance (following the idea of Lawvere reported in \Cref{appendix:lawvere_presheaf_hyperdoctrine}) can be recovered in each fiber by taking ${\top \in \cP(\top\mid\top\mid\top)}$ and ${\le_A \times \top : \cP(A \mid \top \mid A)}$, where indeed the two variables are now separated by their polarity.
\begin{definition}[Directed doctrine]
A \emph{(split) directed doctrine} is defined to be a $(\top,\land,\Rightarrow,\exists^p,\forall^p,\le)$-polarized doctrine, i.e., it satisfies all above conditions.
\end{definition}

  \begin{theorem}[Uniqueness of polarized exponentials]\label{thm:uniqueness_pol_exp}
    Although the condition for polarized exponentials does not fit the scheme for either standard nor relative adjunction, having polarized exponentials is a property, not a structure; we show that any other functor $({-{\Rightarrow'}-}) \iso ({-{\Rightarrow}-})$ by a standard ``uniqueness-of-adjoints"-like argument which we show in \Cref{appendix:uniqueness_polarized_implication}, crucially relying on the no-dinatural-variance condition and the fact that the specific reindexing $\cP(\fulllift{\Delta})$ is a bijection-on-objects, and not \emph{any} functor.
  \end{theorem}
  \begin{remark}\label{thm:global_implication}
    As a special case of ${-{\Rightarrow}-}$ one can pick $N' := \Theta,P' := \Gamma, N = P := \top$ to obtain a functor ${-{\Rightarrow_\varphi}-} : \cP(\Gamma\mid\Delta\mid\Theta)^\op \times \cP(\Theta\mid\Delta\mid\Gamma) \to \cP(\Theta\mid\Delta\mid\Gamma)$ which captures a sort of ``global closed structure'', in the spirit of monoidal fibrations \cite{Shulman2008framed}. This functor captures exactly the structure of formulas used in the syntax in \Cref{fig:syntax:formulas}.
  \end{remark}

\subsection{Examples of directed doctrines}
We present the main model of directed first-order logic, given by interpreting types as preorders:
  \begin{example}[Directed doctrine of preorders]
  The following is a directed doctrine, with $\mathsf{Preord}$ the category of preorders and monotone functions as base (i.e. types/terms):
  \[
  \begin{array}{l}
    \CPreord : \ndp{\Preord}^\op \> \Pos \\
    \CPreord(N \mid D \mid P) := [N^\op \times (D^\op \times D) \times P, \I]_{{\Delta}}, \text{where} \\
      \hspace{1em}\mathsf{Obj} := \text{monotone maps } N^\op \times (D^\op \times D) \times P \> \I, \\
      \hspace{1em} \psi \le \varphi := \forall n\!:\!N,d\!:\!D,p\!:\!P. \psi(n,d,d,p) \le \varphi(n,d,d,p).
  \end{array}
  \]
  The idea for entailments is that of dinatural transformations \cite{Dubuc1970dinatural}, since we rely on the fact that $A_0 =A^\op_0$ have the same objects.
  The no-dinatural-variance condition is satisfied, since $N^\op \times (D^\op \times D) \times P \iso (N \times D)^\op \times \top \times (P \times D)$.\\
  Conjunction $\psi \land \varphi$ is interpreted by the pointwise product of monotone functions in $\I$, and similarly exponentials $\psi \Rightarrow \varphi$ by postcomposing $\ang{\psi^\op,\varphi}$ with $\le_\I : \I^\op \times \I \> \I$. Reindexing on formulas is given by precomposition of monotone functions: given $\eta : N \times D \> N',\delta:D \> D',\rho : P \times D \> P'$ and $\varphi : [N^\op \times (D^\op \times D) \times P, \I]$, \[\cP(\eta,\delta,\rho)(\varphi)(n,d'\!,\!d,p)\!:=\!\varphi(\eta^\op(n,d'),\delta^\op(d),\delta(d),\rho(p,d)).\]
  Polarized quantifiers are given using indexed products and coproducts in $\I$ (i.e., glb and lub), e.g.: given $\varphi : [N^\op \times (D^\op \times D) \times (P \times A), \I]$, $\psi : [N^\op \times ((D \times A)^\op \times (D \times A)) \times P, \I]$ we illustrate the idea with the following examples:
  \[
    \begin{array}{l}
      \exists^+(\varphi)(n,d',d,p) := \coprod_{a \in A} \varphi(n,d',d,p,a) \in \I \\
      \forall^\Delta(\psi)(n,d',d,p) := \prod_{a \in A} \psi(n,(d',a),(d,a),p) \in \I\\
    \end{array}
  \]
  For $\forall^\Delta$ we again rely on $A^{\op}_0 = A_0$, in a way that resembles a decategorification of ends \cite{Loregian2021coend}. Moreover, this doctrine has directed equality, interpreted by formulas
  \[
  \begin{array}{l}
  \le_A : [(\Theta \times A)^\op \times (\Delta^\op \times \Delta) \times (\Gamma \times A), \I] \\ \phantom{\le_A} := ((n,a),(d,d'),(p,a')) \mapsto a \le_A a' \in \I.
  \end{array}
  \]
  and the left-relative adjunction for directed equality is interpreted with the following construction: assume $f : \CPreord(\Theta,\Delta,\Gamma), g : \CPreord(\Theta \times A,\Delta,\Gamma \times A)$, such that
   \[\forall n\!:\!N,d\!:\!D,p\!:\!P. f(n,d,d,p) \le g((n,a),d,d,(p,a)).\]
  (Note that dinatural collapse is applied on $g$.) We show
  \[
    \begin{array}{l}
      \forall (n,a)\!:\!N\times A,d\!:\!D,(p,a')\!:\!P\times A.\\
    \ \ (a \le_A a') \land f(n,d,d,p) \le_\I g((n,a),d,d,(p,a')).
    \end{array}
  \]
  Assuming $a \le a'$, we use monotonicity of $g$ to obtain \[f(n,d,d,p) \le g((n,a),d,d,(p,a)) \le g((n,a),d,d,(p,a'))\] as desired. Rule $\Rulelerefl$ corresponds to $\forall a.\top \le_\I (a \le_A a)$. This proof motivates the syntactic restriction for $a,b$ to appear naturally in the conclusion, since we rely on monotonicity of either $a$ or $a'$ to be able to use the hypothesis; however, by following the proof, one can notice that a weaker restriction could suffice, i.e., by allowing $a \le b$ to be contracted if \emph{at least one} of the variables appears naturally. We choose the current approach in order to simplify the treatment and to mirror the case of proof-relevant dinaturals, in which functoriality on both variables is needed for the hexagon to be stated.
  \end{example}
  \begin{definition}[Syntactic directed doctrine]
    Given a theory $\Sigma$, we define the \emph{syntactic doctrine} $\Syn(\Sigma) : \ndp{\Ctx}^\op \> \Pos$ as the syntactic directed doctrine inductively generated from $\Sigma$, as follows: $\Ctx$ is the category where objects are contexts $\Gamma,\Delta$ and morphisms are term substitutions $\Gamma \vdash [\Delta_1,...,\Delta_n]$, i.e., lists of terms $\Gamma \vdash t : \Delta_i$ for $\Delta_i \in [\Delta_1,...,\Delta_n]$ (and composition is substitution, as in \cite{Pitts1995categorical}), and ${\cP(\Theta\mid\Delta\mid\Gamma) := \set{[\Theta\mid\Delta\mid\Gamma]\ \Phi \propctx}}$, where the poset relation is given by the existence of multi-entailment judgements $\Phi \vdash \Psi$, defined in a similar way as substitutions. The rest of the structure follows directly from the rules of entailments, paying particular attention to the points in which weakenings and dinatural lifts are implicitly used in both the syntax and the semantics.
  \end{definition}
  The following lemma establishes the no-dinatural-variance condition for $\Syn$; since we want to show soundness, the fact that $\Syn$ satisfies this property indicates that directed doctrines also need this requirement.
  \begin{theorem}[$\Syn$ has no-dinatural-variance]
    The syntactic doctrine $\Syn(\Sigma)$ satisfies the no-dinatural-variance condition, i.e., there is a bijection between sets of judgements $\set{[\Theta\mid\Delta\mid\Gamma]\ \varphi\prop} \iso \set{[\Theta,\Delta \mid\,\ectx\,\mid\Gamma,\Delta]\ \varphi\prop}.$
  \end{theorem}
  \begin{proof}
  Straightforward by induction on formulas, using ${\set{{(\Theta,\Delta),{\top}} \vdash A} \iso \set{\Theta,\Delta \vdash A}}$ for $\le_A$ and $P{(s \mid t)}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Set doctrines]
  The $\Set$ doctrine of sets and subsets (e.g., \cite{Maietti2013quotient}) can be lifted to a directed doctrine by precomposing the discrete poset functor $\Set \> \Pos$.
\end{definition}
  \begin{theorem}
  A directed doctrine $\cP : \ndp{\C}^\op\!\>\!\Pos$ induces a doctrine ${{\Downarrow^\op} \< \cP : \C^\op}\!\>\!\Pos$ by precomposing with the functor ${\Downarrow}\!:\!\C\!\to\!\ndp{\C}$ given by $C \mapsto (\top\!\mid\!C\!\mid\!\top)$; intuitively, this captures the non-directed ``sub-logic" consisting only of dinatural contexts. Such doctrine will have conjunction, exponentials, existential/universal quantifiers if the original directed doctrine $\cP$ is equipped with such structure (only quantifiers with dinatural polarity are needed).
  A doctrine can be lifted to a directed one by precomposing with ${\Uparrow}\!:\!\ndp{\C}\!\to\!\C$ given by $(\Theta\!\mid\!\Delta\!\mid\!\Gamma)\!\mapsto\!\Theta \times \Delta \times \Delta \times \Gamma$, satisfying the no-dinatural-variance condition.
  \end{theorem}

\section{Interpretation}\label{sec:interpretation}

In the following we define models for the syntax and sketch the construction of the interpretation of a theory in a doctrine. These steps follow mostly the standard approach of functorial semantics à-la-Lawvere \cite{Lawvere1963functorial,Jacobs1999categorical}.

\begin{definition}
  We denote the (2-)category of directed doctrines and their morphisms as $\mathsf{DDoctrine}$, as in \cite{Maietti2015unifying}.
  A \emph{morphism of directed doctrines} $\cP \to \cP'$ for $\cP : \ndp{\C}^\op \rightarrow \Pos$, $\cP' : \ndp{\D}^\op \rightarrow \Pos$ is defined as a pair $(F,\alpha)$, where $F : \C \to \D$ is a functor that preserves products, exponentials, terminal objects, and $\alpha : P \Rightarrow \ndp{F}^\op \< P'$ is a natural transformation such that each functor $\alpha_{\Theta,\Delta,\Gamma} : P(\Theta\mid\Delta\mid\Gamma) \> P'(F(\Theta),F(\Delta),F(\Gamma))$ for each $\Theta,\Delta,\Gamma$ preserves all the structure (i.e., terminal objects, products, polarized exponentials, polarized quantifiers, directed equality) present in each fiber \cite{Jacobs1999categorical}. For instance, preservation of directed equality means that \[\alpha_{\Theta,\Delta,\Gamma}({\le_A} {\times} \varphi) = \cP'({\iso_{\Theta A}} \mid \id \mid {\iso_{\Gamma A}})({\le'_{F(A)}}{\times} F(\varphi))\] where ${\iso_{AB} := \ang{F(\pi_1),F(\pi_2)}: F(A \times B) \to F(A) \times F(B)}$. A \emph{2-cell} ${(F,\alpha) \Rightarrow (G,\beta)}$ is defined by a natural transformation ${\theta : F \Rightarrow G}$ such that \[{\alpha_{\Theta,\Delta,\Gamma}(p) \le \cP'(\theta_{\Theta,\Delta}\mid\theta_{\Delta}\mid\theta_{\Gamma,\Delta})(\beta_{\Theta,\Delta,\Gamma}(p))}\] holds, where $\theta_{\Theta,\Delta},\theta_{\Gamma,\Delta}$ are defined in the only possible way.
  \end{definition}
  \begin{definition}
    We define the \emph{1-category of theories} $\Theory$ where objects are theories $\Sigma$ and a \emph{morphism of theories} $M : {\Sigma \to \Sigma'}$ is defined as a tuple \[M := ({b : \Sigma_B \to \Sigma'_B},{p:\Sigma_P\to\Sigma'_P},{f:\Sigma_F\to\Sigma'_F})\] of set functions, such that
    \begin{itemize}
    \item $f \< \dom' \iso \dom \< \sem{-}_b$ and $f \< \cod' \iso \cod \< \sem{-}_b$ are isomorphisms of sets,
    \item $\forall e \in \Sigma_E,\sem{\mathsf{actx}(e)}_{b\mathsf{ctx}} \vdash \sem{\mathsf{eqL}(e)}_f = \sem{\mathsf{eqR}(e)}_f : \sem{\mathsf{eqt(e)}}_b$ is syntactically derivable from \Cref{fig:syntax:entailments} using $\Sigma'$ as theory,
    \item $p \< \mathsf{pos}' \iso \mathsf{pos} \< \sem{-}_b$ and $p \< \mathsf{neg}' \iso \mathsf{neg} \< \sem{-}_b$,
    \item $\forall a \in \Sigma_A,[\sem{\mathsf{actx}(a)}_{b\mathsf{ctx}}]\ \sem{\mathsf{hyp}(a)}_{p\mathsf{ctx}} \vdash \sem{\mathsf{conc}(a)}_{p\mathsf{ctx}}$ is syntactically derivable from \Cref{fig:syntax:entailments} using $\Sigma'$ as theory,
    \end{itemize}where we denote the induced translations on full types $\sem{-}_b$ and contexts $\sem{-}_{b\mathsf{ctx}}$, terms $\sem{-}_f$, formulas $\sem{-}_p$ and $\sem{-}_{p\mathsf{ctx}}$.
  \end{definition}
  \begin{theorem}[Initial theory]\label{thm:initiality_empty_theory}
  There is a theory $\emptyset$ defined by always choosing the empty set in \Cref{def:theory}. Clearly $\emptyset$ is the initial object in the category of theories $\Theory$.
  \end{theorem}
  \begin{definition}[Underlying theory]
    Given a directed doctrine $\cP : \ndp{\C}^\op \> \Pos$, we define the \emph{underlying theory} $\Lang(\cP) \in \Theory$ as follows:
    \begin{itemize}
    \item base types $\Sigma_B := \C_0$ the objects of $\C$,
    \item base terms $\Sigma_F := \C_1$ is the set of arrows $\C_0$ of $\C$ with their intuitive $\dom,\cod$ functions;
    \item term equalities $\Sigma_E := \set{(A,\Gamma,s,t)\mid s,t \in \set{\Gamma \vdash t : A}, \sem{t}_f = \sem{s}_f}$ are given by the set of terms formed with $\Sigma_B/\Sigma_F$ as base types/terms for which their interpretation $\sem{-}_f$ induced by the previous points is equal in $\C$.
    \item the set of base formulas $\Sigma_P\!:=\!\coprod_{k\in C_0\times C_0 \times C_0} \cP(k)_0$ is the set of all objects in all fibers ${\cP(\Theta\mid\Gamma\mid\Delta)}$, where ${\mathsf{pos}(p) := \Theta\times\Delta}$ and $\mathsf{neg}(p) := \Gamma\times\Delta$ (more precisely, $\mathsf{pos}(k,p) := k_1 \times k_2$, $\mathsf{neg}$ similarly); this is another crucial point in which we intuitively rely on no-dinatural-variance, because by the isomorphism $\cP(\Theta \mid \Delta \mid \Gamma) \iso \cP(\Theta \times \Delta \mid \top \mid \Gamma \times \Delta)$ the choice of $\mathsf{pos},\mathsf{neg}$ uniquely represents all predicates, without leaving any predicate uncovered.
    \item finally, the set of base axioms $\Sigma_A$ contains a symbol whenever $\psi \le \varphi$ holds for some $\psi,\varphi$ in any poset.
    \end{itemize}
  \end{definition}

  \begin{definition}[Model]
    A \emph{model} of a theory $\Sigma$ in a directed doctrine $\cP$ is a doctrine morphism $\Syn(\cP) \to \Lang(\Sigma)$. Such a doctrine morphism corresponds exactly with the classical notion of model one expects: e.g., in the posetal case, a model prescribes a poset for each base type $\in \Sigma_B$, a monotone function for each term, suitable monotone functions to interpret product types and conjunction formulas, etc.
    \end{definition}
The main theorem below establishes the correspondence between models and syntactic choices (i.e., morphisms of theories): by giving an interpretation of a theory in a doctrine, one can use directed first-order logic to reason about any directed doctrine, where doctrine morphisms now represent a notion of model.
From the main theorem, we establish that directed first-order logic is the sound and complete internal language for directed doctrines.
  \begin{theorem}[Internal language correspondence]\label{thm:main_theorem}
    The above constructions are functorial, forming (2-)functors: \[\Syn : \Theory \rightleftarrows \mathsf{DDoctrine} : \Lang\] Moreover, they form a (bi-)adjunction between (2-)categories, i.e., there are equivalences of categories between the above category and the set below \cite[2.2.5]{Jacobs1999categorical} for any theory $\Sigma$ and directed doctrine $\cP : \ndp{\C}^\op \> \Pos$:
    \[
    \begin{prooftree}
      \hypo{\Syn(\Sigma) \longrightarrow \cP \text{ in $\mathsf{DDoctrine}$}}
      \infer[double]1{\Sigma \longrightarrow \Lang(\cP) \text{ in $\Theory$}}
    \end{prooftree}
    \]
  \end{theorem}
  \begin{proof}
    We start by describing the two constructions, focusing in particular on the case of base predicates, since it is where we need \Cref{def:no_dinat_variance}.
    \\ \emph{(Construction $\Downarrow$)}. One evaluates the doctrine morphism ${(F,\alpha) : \Syn(\Sigma) \longrightarrow \cP}$ at the judgements involving $\Sigma$ to obtain the actions of the following signature morphism:
    \begin{itemize}
      \setlength{\itemindent}{-1em}
    \item $b(\sigma) := F_0(\sigma \type \in \Obj(\Syn(\Sigma)))  \in (\Obj(\C) \equiv \Lang(\cP)_B).$
    \item $f(\sigma) := F_1(\sem{\cod(\sigma)}_b \vdash \sigma(\id) : \sem{\cod(\sigma)}_b) \in \Lang(\cP)_F$. For the induced interpretation one has that ${\sem{A}_b = F(A)}$, $\sem{t}_f = F(t)$ hold by induction on $A,t$, since $F_0,F_1$ pre\-ser\-ve products, exponentials, etc., in each inductive step.
    \item Term equality condition: given $e \in \Sigma_E$, its associated base equality holds in $\Syn(\Sigma)$ therefore the terms/arrows are equal. Functors $F$ preserve equalities of arrows into $\cP$, hence their $\sem{-}_f$ is equal in $\Lang(\cP)_E$ by definition.
    \item For predicates, ${p(\sigma) := (F(\mathsf{pos}(\sigma)),F(\top),F(\mathsf{neg}(\sigma)),\tilde p)}$ $\in \Lang(\cP)_F$, where the point $\tilde p :=\alpha(\sigma(\pi_1,\pi_1) \prop)$ $\in {\cP(F(\mathsf{pos}(\sigma)) \mid F(\top) \mid F(\mathsf{neg}(\sigma)))_0}$ is obtained by applying $\alpha$ to the base case $P(s \mid t) \prop$, but picking $\Delta := \top$ and the projections $\mathsf{pos}(\sigma), \top \vdash \pi_1 : \mathsf{pos}(\sigma)$ for $s,t$. This choice essentially corresponds to $\fulllift{\top}$. Moreover, \[{\textsf{pos}'(\tilde p) := F(\mathsf{pos}(\sigma)) \times F(\top) \iso F(\textsf{pos}(\sigma)) = \sem{\textsf{pos}(\sigma)}_b}.\] This $\iso$ could be resolved more precisely by working with a weaker 2-$\Cat$ of theories.
    \item For axioms, we apply monotonicity of $\alpha$ on the relation $\mathsf{hyp}(a) \vdash \mathsf{conc}(a)$ in $\Syn$, which holds by the axiom case for $\sigma \in \Sigma_F$. From this we obtain that the desired $\le$ relation in $\cP$, which is exactly how $\Lang(\Sigma)_A$ was defined.
    \\ \emph{(Construction $\Uparrow$)}. Each component of the doctrine morphism $(\sem{-},\sem{-}_\varphi)$ is given by induction on derivations, using the theory morphism $M : \Sigma \to \Lang(\cP)$ for the base cases and the structure of $\cP$ for the inductive steps. The functor $\sem{-} : \set{A \type}_\Sigma \to \C_0$ is defined as:
    \item $\sem{-}_0$ acts by induction on types, using the product functor ${{-\times-} : \Cop\x\C\>\C}$ to interpret product types, etc., using for base types ${A \in \Sigma_B}$ the action $M_b(A) \in \C_0$.
    \item $\sem{-}_1$ acts by induction on terms, similarly as above. Functoriality is ensured by a substitution lemma.
    \item The components of the natural transformation \[{\sem{\Theta\mid\Delta\mid\Gamma}_\varphi : \Syn(\Theta\mid\Delta\mid\Gamma) \> \cP(\sem{\Theta}\mid\sem{\Delta}\mid\sem{\Gamma})}\] are the functors which, on objects, act by induction on formula derivations in $\Syn$ and use the structure of $\cP$, e.g., using $\Conjunction$, implication $\Implication$, (\Cref{thm:global_implication}), and directed equality in $\cP$ to combine objects in the inductive step. For the base judgement $\gamma := \sigma(s \mid t) \prop$ with $\Theta,\Delta \vdash s : \sem{\mathsf{neg}(\sigma)}$, take $\tilde P := p(\sigma \in \Sigma_P) \in \cP(\Theta',\Delta',\Gamma')$. By the no-dinatural-variance condition, $\eps(\tilde P)\in\cP(\Theta'\times\Delta'=:\mathsf{neg'}(b(\sigma)) \iso \sem{\mathsf{neg}(\sigma)}\mid\top\mid\cdots)$.
    Finally, \[\sem{\gamma}_{\varphi} := \cP(\sem{s} \mid {!} \mid \sem{t})(\varepsilon(\tilde P))\in\cP(\sem{\Theta}\mid\sem{\Delta}\mid\sem{\Gamma}).\]
    Naturality is a semantic substitution lemma, by induction.
    \item The action on morphisms (entailments) is given by interpreting each rule with the properties of $\cP$.
    \end{itemize}
    We show the equivalence with details in \Cref{appendix:details_bijection} for base predicates; both directions use the fact that $\varepsilon$ is a bijection.
    \begin{itemize}
       \item \emph{(${{\Downarrow}\<{\Uparrow}={\id}}$).} The no-dinatural-variance condition is used here with naturality of $\alpha$ to cover all the predicates in each fiber given by the original interpretation.
      \item \emph{(${{\Uparrow}\<{\Downarrow}\!=\!{\id}}$).} Similarly straightforward since the interpretation $\sem{-}$ is essentially determined (by induction) via the action on the base predicates of $M$.
    \end{itemize}
    \vspace{-1.35em}
  \end{proof}

  The following corollaries establish soundness (i.e., an initiality theorem \cite{Pitts1995categorical}) and completeness for the syntax.
  \begin{corollary}[Soundness]
    If an entailment is derivable in directed first-order logic on the empty theory, it holds in every directed doctrine.
  \end{corollary}
  \begin{proof}
    By \Cref{thm:initiality_empty_theory}, $\emptyset$ is initial in $\Theory$, therefore the bottom, and top, sets in \Cref{thm:main_theorem} are singletons, hence $\Syn(\emptyset)$ is also initial. A doctrine morphism $\Syn(\emptyset) \longrightarrow \cP$ is exactly a model of the empty theory in $\cP$, hence we can send the entailment to the one internal to $\cP$.
  \end{proof}
  \begin{corollary}[Completeness]
    \!\!If an entailment holds in all directed doctrines, it is derivable in directed logic.
  \end{corollary}
  \begin{proof}
    Since $\Syn(\emptyset)$ is a directed doctrine, the statement is true also in $\Syn(\emptyset)$. By definition of $\Syn(\emptyset)$, a statement is true internally precisely when it is derivable.
  \end{proof}
\section{Conclusion and Future Work}\label{sec:conclusions}
In this paper we introduced a sound and complete syntax for a directed first-order proof-irrelevant type theory with polarities and a ``directed'' notion of equality $\le_A$, which we characterized in terms of a left relative adjoint. This treatment of directed-relations-as-equality further enlightens a question first posed by Lawvere on the precise role of $\hom$ and variance \cite{Lawvere1970equality} in the categorical treatment of logic, treated here for the case of preorders.

The concept of polarized contexts described in this paper is a further step towards a satisfactory syntactic treatment of directed type theory, which even in the first-order case with preorder semantics is particularly non-trivial due to the treatment of polarity. A possible generalization would be to incorporate type dependency and use a notion of indexed posets as models: however, it is not clear what role variance should play when variables appear in the types themselves. Following the geometric intuition for HoTT, the theory of directed spaces and directed algebraic topology \cite{Fajstrup2016directed} might prove to be a suitable model for (an extension) of our logic that can reason about concurrent systems, since directed equality lends itself particularly well to be interpreted as a sort of rewrite as in rewriting logic \cite{Meseguer2012twenty}, for which we investigated here just the universal property. Syntactically, we saw how directed first-order logic is a only a slight departure from first-order logic, allowing for it to be expanded in the same way that the latter is the basis of many logical systems. Another approach to capturing variances could be based on a multicategorical ``graded" approach \cite{Levy2019locally}, tagging variables with polarities and using a binary operation that combines the polarity of variables together such that $- \otimes + = \Delta$. A relatively straightfoward but important extension to this work is given by having a type $A^\op$ for any type $A$ as in \cite{Licata20112}: our work lays the foundation for such a treatment, since we can state precisely how a positive occurrence of $A^\textsf{op}$ is equivalent to a negative occurrence of $A$ (hence $\op$-types simply swap positive and negative contexts), and such $-^\textsf{op}$ operation becomes a "representable" way (in a sense similar to \cite{Shulman2018contravariance}) to express such swap of polarities between positive and negative variables.

