Through a comprehensive evaluation of our STT and Non-Spec\-ulative Data Access (NDA) microarchitectural designs on the RISC-V Berkeley Out-of-Order Machine, we find that the IPC impact of in-core secure schemes is higher than previously estimated, close to 20\% for the highest performance core. 
With insights into timing from our RTL evaluation, the performance loss, created by the combined impact of IPC and timing, becomes even greater, at 35\%, 27\%, and 22\% for STT-Rename, STT-Issue, and NDA, respectively.
If these trends were to hold for leading processor core designs, the performance impact would be well over 30\%, even for the best-performing scheme.

Through these findings, research sentiments of Spectre being solvable by in-core secure schemes at low performance costs are challenged.
\name{} serves as a call to arms for more in-depth evaluation of secure speculation schemes, and further work into in-core methods and optimizations, which can help mitigate the high performance cost of current state-of-the-art schemes, without requiring extensive and expensive modifications.
