Correctly and effectively determining the youngest-root-of-taint (YRoT) for an instruction is challenging. % in hardware.
YRoT is functionally a mechanism of DIFT~\cite{dift:ASPLOS2004}, which aims to propagate information about the dependency chains of certain instructions.
In the case of YRoT, the goal is to always know the youngest speculative load that a given instruction depends on, directly, or indirectly.
Instructions become non-speculative chronologically, i.e., a younger instruction will always become non-speculative later or at the same point as an older instruction.
When the youngest load that an instruction depends on is non-speculative, the associated instruction can no longer depend on speculative data, and can be executed securely. 

Dependencies are well understood in out-of-order processors, which employ register renaming to remove ambiguity about which earlier instruction a given instruction depends on.
Mapping an architectural \emph{destination} register to a physical register is trivial, and can be performed in isolation without considering any other instructions.
