Performing the YRoT computation at instruction issue \circled{1} means that the issued instruction might still be tainted, requiring for the instruction to be killed \circled{2} and replayed.
Since back-to-back issuing of instructions is crucial for performance, high-performance architectures speculatively issue instructions that depend on a load, with the assumption that the load will hit in the level-one cache.
On a cache miss, the speculatively issued instruction must be replayed~\cite{scheduling-replay:HPCA2004}.
Thus, contemporary high-performance cores already have functionality for replying instructions, which can be reused to also replay tainted instructions.
